00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.803
So Sarah?

00:04:43.004 --> 00:04:45.680
- Thank you so much, Priya,
for that kind introduction

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.230
and very generous
invitation for me to speak

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:52.430
at this exciting
interdisciplinary colloquium.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:55.010
It's an incredible
opportunity to sort of reflect

00:04:55.010 --> 00:05:00.010
on the field of computation and
theory in molecular biology.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:03.993
And I'm really excited
to be giving this talk.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.760
So I've called it "The
Inference of Nature"

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.380
because what we're inferring
in computational biology,

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:13.740
theoretical biology, bioinformatics

00:05:14.742 --> 00:05:19.742
are molecules and cellular
components of organisms

00:05:23.624 --> 00:05:24.774
and their interactions.

00:05:25.726 --> 00:05:30.190
And so it's essentially
prediction of features of nature

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:33.620
kind of at that level of
the individual components.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:36.380
And within theoretical
and computational biology

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:41.330
at this molecular level, they are,

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.823
one of the main aims is of course,

00:05:45.185 --> 00:05:47.840
to predict causation from correlation.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.181
And there are really two
different schools of thought here.

00:05:51.181 --> 00:05:55.001
One is that you want to get
the mechanistic details right.

00:05:55.001 --> 00:05:56.570
So if molecule A interacts with molecule B

00:05:56.570 --> 00:06:01.273
interacts with molecule C and
causes a pathway or cascade

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.470
of biological interactions and processes,

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.390
then you wanna be for that you're,

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:12.290
that you're modeling the
sequence in the correct way

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:14.780
and the feed forward and feed back loops

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:17.810
and individual interactions

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:22.180
and the directions of these
arrows all precisely correct

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:26.804
and ideally with quantitative
kinetics and so on

00:06:26.804 --> 00:06:29.470
attached to the, as labels
to these interactions.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:34.420
And so even though A may correlate with C,

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:37.500
you'd wanna make sure that the modeling

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.810
is modeling the correct
sequence of A to B to C

00:06:41.981 --> 00:06:43.330
and not sort of jumping ahead of itself.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:47.670
And this school of theoretical
and computational biology,

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:50.420
this school of modeling
is really concerned

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:54.739
with these mechanistic
details and describing them

00:06:54.739 --> 00:06:57.585
with differential
equations and all sorts of,

00:06:57.585 --> 00:07:00.090
well, you know, Boolean
models and related methods

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:05.090
that sort of fit into
that level of biology.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.930
But there's another school of thought

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:11.910
that's really more
concerned with correlation

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:15.860
rather than those mechanistic details

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:17.843
and for very good reason.

00:07:18.889 --> 00:07:23.500
And that is that, you know,
we can calculate linear,

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:28.500
non-linear, all different,
you know, correlations

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.090
in any numbers of dimensions.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:33.230
Shown here is a very simple
correlation between A and B.

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:34.970
And often we're doing that,

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:38.353
not between sort of two simple molecules

00:07:38.353 --> 00:07:40.730
and a few data points, but
in absolutely huge data sets.

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:43.130
And the data sets that I'm showing here

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:47.250
are actually quite modest on
the scale of what's available

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:50.200
to biology in this day and age

00:07:51.571 --> 00:07:55.048
and for many different
types of measurements.

00:07:55.048 --> 00:07:56.993
And the field that I'm
most active at the moment

00:07:56.993 --> 00:07:58.608
is genomics and functional genomics

00:07:58.608 --> 00:08:01.710
where we're measuring, for
instance, as an example,

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:03.890
the mRNA levels, the expression levels

00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:05.560
of all the genes in the genome

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:08.069
in millions of cells at the same time.

00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:10.590
And so the size of these data
sets are absolutely huge.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.820
And we can calculate the
relationships between

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:15.810
or the correlations and relationships

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:19.310
between individual
genes and represent them

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:20.210
as these networks.

00:08:21.229 --> 00:08:22.620
For instance, that's
one way of mining data.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:26.440
We can also collapse high dimensional data

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:29.240
that's in 20,000 dimensions
into a two dimensional space.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:31.925
And what's shown here
on the right-hand side

00:08:31.925 --> 00:08:33.506
are individual cells,

00:08:33.506 --> 00:08:36.310
and you can see how there are
hundreds of thousands of them

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:38.220
projected into this
little, this little panel

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.207
on the right-hand side here,

00:08:40.207 --> 00:08:41.160
where I'm showing my red cursor.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:43.550
And so the way we,

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:45.600
the only way to really
tackle these data sets

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.440
is using statistical
and computational tools

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:51.690
from data science, as
well as machine learning

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:53.650
and artificial, you know,

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:55.988
more globally artificial
intelligence methods,

00:08:55.988 --> 00:08:58.613
deep learning and so on because
the magnitude of the data

00:08:58.613 --> 00:09:00.900
is simply so enormous that we can't,

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:03.370
it doesn't make sense to tackle it

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:07.210
with differential equation-based systems

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:09.091
in mechanistic models.

00:09:09.091 --> 00:09:12.225
And what we're extracting
are correlations if you will,

00:09:12.225 --> 00:09:17.225
in these very large data sets
in order to extract principles

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.968
of the molecules, their interactions.

00:09:20.968 --> 00:09:22.510
On the right-hand side,

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:26.470
it's actually molecular
fingerprints of cells.

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:29.410
So it's basically features

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:34.040
of these biological building
blocks from large data.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:39.040
And really what I'm trying
to say is that, you know,

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:42.530
the correlation really
or these relationships

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:45.620
in this data space are informative.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:48.600
And sometimes we don't really care

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:51.822
whether they're causal or not.

00:09:51.822 --> 00:09:53.760
It's just, the correlation is
enough to make a prediction.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.060
And so what I'm trying
to say is for instance,

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:58.330
red sky at night, shepherd's delight.

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:00.593
Red sky in the morning,
shepherd's warning.

00:10:00.593 --> 00:10:02.429
That little ditty about the weather

00:10:02.429 --> 00:10:04.190
and that you can predict the weather

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:06.150
from the color of the sky is, you know,

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:11.150
it's a very powerful,
predictive principle,

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.670
but of course the color at night

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:15.360
doesn't cause the weather the next day,

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:17.810
but the correlation is good
enough to be very powerful.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:19.620
And that in itself,

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:22.828
sometimes predictions in and of themselves

00:10:22.828 --> 00:10:25.490
are really what we care about,

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:27.249
even if we don't understand

00:10:27.249 --> 00:10:31.812
the detailed mechanistic reasons,

00:10:31.812 --> 00:10:36.812
we haven't deciphered every
single causal, you know,

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:38.760
element in the process.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.849
- [Priya] So Sarah, if I may
ask for a quick clarification.

00:10:41.849 --> 00:10:42.682
- Sure.

00:10:42.682 --> 00:10:46.670
- In the sort of multi, highly
multidimensional spaces,

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:48.060
what is predicated on the fact

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:51.400
that you know all the
variables in question, right?

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:54.130
You know, what the variables are.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:57.570
Or is there an element of also looking

00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:59.513
for what might be potential?

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:01.609
And I ask only

00:11:01.609 --> 00:11:04.223
because it's such a huge
multidimensional space.

00:11:05.173 --> 00:11:06.909
- So it's a great question.

00:11:06.909 --> 00:11:09.130
I think, you know, this
comes back to the, sort of,

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:12.530
let's say the, you know, the thinking that

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:14.010
is data mining unbiased?

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:16.307
And of course the, you know,

00:11:16.307 --> 00:11:18.220
these methods that I'm
showing here for calculation

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:21.540
of graphs between molecules
from high dimensional,

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:24.370
this is from high dimensional
single cell genomics data

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:26.570
or manifold projection,
you know, which is,

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:30.854
this is a universal manifold
approximate projection here

00:11:30.854 --> 00:11:31.740
that I'm showing on the right-hand side.

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:34.210
These are, these are unbiased,

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:37.310
but in a way in doing these calculations,

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:42.310
we are, we have, as the
scientist, you know,

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:46.790
we have a sort of a certain hypothesis

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:51.400
or a certain mental framework

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:56.010
that drives us to actually
simplify and project the data

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:59.113
in this way, even if the
computational methods are unbiased.

00:12:00.007 --> 00:12:02.440
That's getting a little
theoretical and philosophical.

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:05.210
But for instance, what I'm
showing here is a graph

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:07.800
calculated through the correlation
of transcription factors.

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:10.470
And as such, it's completely unbiased.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:13.090
That's a completely
hypothesis free method,

00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:16.150
but actually my hypothesis, you know,

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:20.200
I have a sort of hypothesis
that I haven't articulated here.

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:23.230
And that is that the subset
of genes that I'm showing here

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:25.410
are transcriptional regulators.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:28.510
They are the class of genes
that switch other genes

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:29.630
on and off.

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:31.840
And so the reason that
we did this analysis

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:34.268
was that our hypothesis is

00:12:34.268 --> 00:12:36.244
that these are the key regulatory factors

00:12:36.244 --> 00:12:37.908
that determine these cell states.

00:12:37.908 --> 00:12:38.950
And that's why we're calculating

00:12:38.950 --> 00:12:41.600
the correlation between
expression between, you know.

00:12:42.507 --> 00:12:44.170
- And you have confidence.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:47.483
- So I think there's always
a sort of yin and yang in a,

00:12:52.043 --> 00:12:53.370
you know, data mining.

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:56.390
There's always a, there
isn't a contradiction

00:12:56.390 --> 00:12:59.863
between the hypothesis
driven mechanistic modeling

00:12:59.863 --> 00:13:03.340
and the unbiased

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:06.413
quote, unquote data
science kind of approach,

00:13:07.479 --> 00:13:08.880
because actually they're not so,

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:10.485
they're not completely different worlds

00:13:10.485 --> 00:13:12.990
because actually there's
often a hypothesis

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:15.950
or a sort of framework
that you start from,

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:18.220
even if that ends up, you know, surprising

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:20.460
and it's not correct and
you discover new things

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:23.760
using the unbiased machine
learning or data mining tools.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:24.850
Does that make sense?

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:25.683
- Yeah, thanks.

00:13:28.554 --> 00:13:32.405
- So that really, your question
really comes back to this

00:13:32.405 --> 00:13:36.761
sort of supposed dichotomy
between the modelers

00:13:36.761 --> 00:13:39.910
who do mechanistic modeling
and the data miners,

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:43.423
and, you know, are they,
completely different, you know,

00:13:44.317 --> 00:13:46.215
coming from completely
different ends of the world?

00:13:46.215 --> 00:13:47.219
No, they're not.

00:13:47.219 --> 00:13:48.660
Actually, you know, both
of them are scientists

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:52.040
who, you know, start
from certain hypotheses.

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:55.540
Yes, the data mining approach can seem

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:58.110
completely hypothesis free and unbiased,

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:01.360
but actually, you know, you always start

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.617
from a certain way of analyzing the data

00:14:04.617 --> 00:14:06.330
which in itself implies a hypothesis.

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:08.150
You then discover components,

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:11.400
which in turn can enter the
mechanistic models and so on.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:12.570
So at the end of the day,

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:14.440
there's really a productive symbiosis

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:15.810
between these different approaches

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:19.293
within the domain of theoretical
and computational biology.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:25.480
And of course, in molecular
and cellular biology,

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:31.200
we are in a luxurious situation

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:34.880
where we can actually interrogate
the systems experimentally

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:36.220
that we're studying.

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:41.000
So we're not in a, operating
in a domain like climate change

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:44.450
and climate modeling where we can't tinker

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:48.570
with one variable, one factor
and see what the outcome is

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:49.960
of doing that perturbation.

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:53.000
Or let's say the cosmos,
you know, that, of course,

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.980
my friend Priya works in where, you know,

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:57.390
it's very difficult to
eliminate one planet

00:14:57.390 --> 00:15:00.240
or something like that
and see what changes.

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.080
In biology, that's not the case.

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:03.180
We can actually do experiments.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:07.900
And so it's, you know, and that in a way

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:12.440
that means that the
modeling is, has always been

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:14.950
part of the,

00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:18.190
or the theoretical and
computational approaches

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:21.400
and modeling and inference have
always been part of biology

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:22.233
to some extent.

00:15:22.233 --> 00:15:24.440
And one example is genetics.

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:27.048
And what I'm showing
here is Gregor Mendel,

00:15:27.048 --> 00:15:32.048
the monk who observed the
peas and the variation

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:37.670
in color size and shape and
flowers and so on of the peas,

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:39.500
according to their inheritance

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:43.180
and from the patterns that he observed

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:47.300
in terms of the offspring's features,

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:49.830
he inferred that there
must be the existence

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:53.313
of inherited factors that
came to be known as genes.

00:15:54.418 --> 00:15:56.880
And so this, the earliest
genetics experiments

00:15:57.835 --> 00:16:02.760
that are relating changes to
genetic crosses, you know,

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:05.239
are making an inference that it's,

00:16:05.239 --> 00:16:08.720
the stroke of genius that he
did was to make this inference

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:11.323
that there are factors that
are inheriting these features.

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:15.293
What I also mentioned
is that some, you know,

00:16:16.606 --> 00:16:17.676
in cell biology,

00:16:17.676 --> 00:16:18.878
and this is a case of
developmental biology,

00:16:18.878 --> 00:16:20.128
so embryonic development,

00:16:22.834 --> 00:16:24.550
there are other kinds of perturbations.

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:27.960
So the genetic perturbation
is basically variation

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:32.430
that's the perturbation in
here in developmental biology.

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:37.087
It's basically taking a bunch
of cells or a tissue section

00:16:39.395 --> 00:16:41.395
and transplanting it into another embryo

00:16:42.417 --> 00:16:46.630
and showing that you can
control access formation

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:50.860
through that region called the organizer,

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:55.403
that region of tissue that
acts on a donor tissue,

00:16:56.996 --> 00:16:58.000
on a host tissue,

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:01.850
the host tissue takes on the
features of the donor tissue.

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:06.350
And so that kind of, that's
another kind of perturbation.

00:17:06.350 --> 00:17:08.233
The third kind of more modern,

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:13.490
getting towards more high
throughput experiments in genetics

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:16.800
is exemplified by this classic experiment

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:20.036
by Nusslein-Volhard and Wieschaus

00:17:20.036 --> 00:17:23.900
where they did chemical
perturbations in Drosophila embryos

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:27.040
to uncover the genes that
control the patterning

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:28.740
of the embryo, where you get these

00:17:29.778 --> 00:17:31.730
beautiful striped-like
patterns that are controlled

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:34.010
through a hierarchical set of genes

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:36.998
that determine the polarity,

00:17:36.998 --> 00:17:39.222
the gaps, and the smaller stripes.

00:17:39.222 --> 00:17:42.090
And ultimately these iconic homeotic genes

00:17:43.256 --> 00:17:45.299
that control the anterior,
posterior access.

00:17:45.299 --> 00:17:47.150
So it's, and this was through

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.763
systematic chemical
mutagenesis and observation,

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:54.402
by Nusslein-Volhard and Wieschaus.

00:17:54.402 --> 00:17:55.235
So we have these,

00:17:56.191 --> 00:17:58.903
the genetics basically and
perturbation experiments.

00:18:00.015 --> 00:18:02.880
And we also have, what's
more the molecular

00:18:03.719 --> 00:18:05.470
and biochemical level of experiments

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:07.440
in terms of individual molecules,

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:11.320
and what I wanna use
to exemplify this here,

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:15.380
where inference plays a role
at multiple different levels

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:17.917
is making molecular models.

00:18:17.917 --> 00:18:20.900
So making models of the
three-dimensional structure

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:21.800
of molecules.

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:25.580
And of course the legendary
example is double helix,

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:30.580
where you had two different
approaches to tackle that.

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:31.963
On the one hand,

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:36.170
the careful crystal graphic
experimental approach

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:38.750
pursued by the group in London

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:43.750
and the Franklin and
Gosling paper that publishes

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:46.280
the x-ray diffraction pattern of crystals

00:18:47.573 --> 00:18:51.343
of deoxyribonucleic acid.

00:18:52.405 --> 00:18:54.402
And then of course, the double helix

00:18:54.402 --> 00:18:57.957
is inferred computationally
from the diffraction pattern.

00:18:57.957 --> 00:18:59.220
And that in itself is a kind of inference

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:03.010
in the sense that you're
calculating or inferring

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:06.160
what the three-dimensional
structure of the atoms must be

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:09.170
from the x-ray diffraction
pattern measurements.

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:13.160
So there you can see that
the experimental measurement

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:15.400
really relies on a computation.

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:18.280
It's not like Gregor Mendel's peas,

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:21.063
where he's simply looking
at the color with his eyes.

00:19:21.998 --> 00:19:25.197
Here, in order to interpret
this very complex data,

00:19:25.197 --> 00:19:27.036
this x-ray diffraction pattern,

00:19:27.036 --> 00:19:32.036
and then sort of predict
the double helical structure

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:37.337
from this pattern, what's
required is computations,

00:19:37.337 --> 00:19:42.203
and that require a computer basically.

00:19:43.599 --> 00:19:47.340
And on the other hand
then, Watson and Crick,

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:48.766
of course,

00:19:48.766 --> 00:19:51.350
used a combination of
different pieces of evidence

00:19:52.562 --> 00:19:54.012
to build the model in a more,

00:19:55.242 --> 00:19:58.500
in a more conventional way
with actual physical models

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:01.370
by using the information
from the diffraction pattern

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:02.203
and the step,

00:20:03.049 --> 00:20:05.092
the distance between the steps
and the rungs of the ladder

00:20:05.092 --> 00:20:07.680
or the base pairs in the DNA double helix,

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:09.760
combined with chemical information

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:14.013
about the complimentary
(audio blurs) A T and G and C,

00:20:15.437 --> 00:20:17.410
and a few other pieces of evidence.

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:20.110
And then they stitched together

00:20:20.110 --> 00:20:23.210
these orthogonal pieces of evidence

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:25.270
to come up with a double helical model.

00:20:25.270 --> 00:20:28.475
So there are two different
types of inference here.

00:20:28.475 --> 00:20:30.560
One is the calculations on
the experimental measurement,

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:34.634
and the other is basically
putting together different pieces

00:20:34.634 --> 00:20:35.467
of experimental data.

00:20:35.467 --> 00:20:38.395
But the point that I
wanna bring across here

00:20:38.395 --> 00:20:42.190
is that the experimental data
was key to make the models.

00:20:42.190 --> 00:20:46.050
And at the same time, the
model had to be validated

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:47.120
by experimental data.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:51.973
So it's this molecular
structure prediction,

00:20:51.973 --> 00:20:53.491
which I'll also talk about later

00:20:53.491 --> 00:20:56.220
is very closely intertwined

00:20:56.220 --> 00:20:59.650
with the observations from experiments

00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:03.090
being coupled with different
types of inference,

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:08.090
computational, and more
intellectual sort of model,

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:12.091
and this challenge of
predicting protein structure.

00:21:12.091 --> 00:21:14.972
And we've, you know,
Priya just before the talk

00:21:14.972 --> 00:21:17.660
raised the question of AlphaFold,
which of course has been,

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:20.130
you know, very widely publicized

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:23.080
as this deep learning approach

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:25.233
for systematic structural prediction.

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:29.199
This has been a challenge basically

00:21:29.199 --> 00:21:33.650
from when the first protein
sequences were sequenced

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:36.803
using sort of Sanger, Sanger sequencing.

00:21:40.665 --> 00:21:45.390
And, you know, the simple
little sequence of insulin

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:49.100
for instance, had to wait for many years,

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:53.416
until Dorothy Crowfoot Hodgkin
solved the crystal structure.

00:21:53.416 --> 00:21:56.950
And so this, because of
the powerful information

00:21:56.950 --> 00:21:59.971
that's inherent in these
molecular structures,

00:21:59.971 --> 00:22:01.874
you just saw the double helix,

00:22:01.874 --> 00:22:04.017
which is a very clear one, because the,

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:08.180
that basically then
immediately gave the clue

00:22:09.177 --> 00:22:11.950
that the genetic code consists
of this sequence of letters,

00:22:11.950 --> 00:22:15.591
A, T, G, and C for the proteins,

00:22:15.591 --> 00:22:17.500
which consists of a 20 letter alphabet.

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:19.870
It's perhaps a bit more difficult to see

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:21.310
what's so important

00:22:21.310 --> 00:22:23.210
about knowing these molecular structures,

00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:25.970
but basically you'll
just have to bear with me

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:27.880
and believe me when I
say that this structure

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:29.210
also gives a lot of information

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:31.120
about the function of the protein

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:34.040
and what its interaction
partners could be in,

00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:35.740
and so on and so forth.

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:38.490
And so this exercise in critical,

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:41.757
in protein structure prediction,
which has been, you know,

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:48.010
a competition in the
community on an annual basis

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:50.060
to assess or benchmark

00:22:50.060 --> 00:22:52.543
what the best structure
prediction methods are,

00:22:53.399 --> 00:22:55.670
it's been going on for
many, many, for, you know,

00:22:55.670 --> 00:22:58.200
14 or more iterations,

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:01.886
has in a way culminated
in this deep learning.

00:23:01.886 --> 00:23:04.136
And the reason for that
is that this approach

00:23:05.346 --> 00:23:07.710
can now at this juncture in our history

00:23:07.710 --> 00:23:11.080
draw on such rich experimental data sets,

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:13.460
where there are on the order
of hundreds of thousands

00:23:13.460 --> 00:23:14.992
of individual protein structures

00:23:14.992 --> 00:23:17.668
that the rules for making
these proteins structures

00:23:17.668 --> 00:23:20.431
can now be learned in an automatic way.

00:23:20.431 --> 00:23:22.230
And that wasn't possible in the early days

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:23.730
when there was much less data,

00:23:24.726 --> 00:23:28.146
but there are many areas of
biology for which that's true.

00:23:28.146 --> 00:23:30.190
So the protein data bank is the repository

00:23:30.190 --> 00:23:31.310
of crystal structures,

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:36.284
the three-dimensional atomic
coordinates of molecules,

00:23:36.284 --> 00:23:38.124
proteins like I just showed you,

00:23:38.124 --> 00:23:40.388
and that's been going on for 50 years now,

00:23:40.388 --> 00:23:43.160
and it's this incredible
big data resource.

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:47.407
What I'm showing here are
other databases in biology

00:23:47.407 --> 00:23:51.303
the protein sequences and
UniProt, genes and genomes

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:56.720
and Ensembl, and then
down at the bottom two,

00:23:56.720 --> 00:24:01.290
sort of more functional genomics databases

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:04.621
that I'm involved with the
Human Cell Atlas Data Portal

00:24:04.621 --> 00:24:06.440
and the EBI Single Cell Expression Atlas

00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:11.440
that are portals for the single
cell genomics data that the,

00:24:14.557 --> 00:24:16.304
in particular the gene expression data

00:24:16.304 --> 00:24:17.723
at the level of single cells,

00:24:18.783 --> 00:24:19.730
which is being possible

00:24:19.730 --> 00:24:23.130
through a resolution revolution genomics

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:26.777
that allows us to measure the
expression of single cells

00:24:26.777 --> 00:24:28.303
I'll come to later in my talk.

00:24:28.303 --> 00:24:29.610
- [Man] I haven't heard of UniProt.

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:30.443
What is that?

00:24:31.683 --> 00:24:35.983
- UniProt is the protein
sequence database,

00:24:35.983 --> 00:24:37.983
ao the amino acid sequences of proteins.

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:41.916
Yeah, so my point here was really

00:24:41.916 --> 00:24:42.940
that there are a lot of databases now

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:47.940
that provide the substrate
for data mining in biology.

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:53.410
And this is really, you
know, is a development

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:54.963
that's gone on over decades,

00:24:55.856 --> 00:24:57.520
but it's really accelerated
over the past few years

00:24:57.520 --> 00:25:00.500
that we're now in the era
of big data in biology.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:03.500
And that's, there's absolutely
no question about that.

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:07.579
It's, you know, an
exponential increase in data

00:25:07.579 --> 00:25:08.530
that's happening at the moment.

00:25:08.530 --> 00:25:11.640
And there are a range of
theoretical numerical approaches

00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:13.518
that aid inference.

00:25:13.518 --> 00:25:15.422
And I've said on the one
hand there's modeling.

00:25:15.422 --> 00:25:17.239
There are also in silico simulations,

00:25:17.239 --> 00:25:19.180
in silico representations of systems

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:22.230
where you can perturb systems
completely theoretically.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:27.230
And so in contrast to these
modeling simulation approaches,

00:25:27.642 --> 00:25:29.813
there are data science approaches.

00:25:30.700 --> 00:25:32.530
And some of these are
when the data on its own

00:25:32.530 --> 00:25:36.341
is self-evident, like in to
Gregor Mendel, but there are,

00:25:36.341 --> 00:25:38.093
there are also these
larger scale approaches,

00:25:43.847 --> 00:25:47.022
and yeah, these for AlphaFold,
it's the structures,

00:25:47.022 --> 00:25:50.099
and with these big data approaches now,

00:25:50.099 --> 00:25:53.710
the methods are really
statistical, computational,

00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:55.860
machine learning and
AI, as I've mentioned.

00:25:57.080 --> 00:26:02.080
So, you know, given that
there is this very big

00:26:03.050 --> 00:26:07.200
and important field of
computational biology

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:09.330
in molecular biology,

00:26:10.229 --> 00:26:14.774
so that there can be a situation
where this field, you know,

00:26:14.774 --> 00:26:17.660
which wasn't, you know, a
traditional part of biology,

00:26:17.660 --> 00:26:20.980
this field of computational
biology and using big data

00:26:20.980 --> 00:26:25.980
to analyze and predict,
feed biological structures,

00:26:28.266 --> 00:26:29.878
biological interactions,
molecular interactions,

00:26:29.878 --> 00:26:32.770
and so on has meant that
some experimentalists

00:26:32.770 --> 00:26:37.210
might view the theoretical
component of molecular biology

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:38.043
with suspicions.

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:41.730
Okay, and I'm gonna pick up right here

00:26:41.730 --> 00:26:43.943
and dive into the main meat of my talk,

00:26:44.875 --> 00:26:46.670
which is going to be three elements.

00:26:46.670 --> 00:26:50.842
One is talking about predicting pathways

00:26:50.842 --> 00:26:52.363
approaching complex assembly,

00:26:52.363 --> 00:26:54.900
which is in that area of
predicting molecular structure.

00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:58.202
The second is predicting cell types

00:26:58.202 --> 00:26:59.760
using single cell genomics data.

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:02.080
And the third is the Human Cell Atlas

00:27:02.080 --> 00:27:04.160
and using Human Cell Atlas data

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:06.430
to predict cell
communication and infection

00:27:07.302 --> 00:27:09.103
by COVID-19 SARS-CoV-2.

00:27:11.304 --> 00:27:12.965
So if you think about
the inside of a cell,

00:27:12.965 --> 00:27:14.638
and the reason why it's important

00:27:14.638 --> 00:27:16.903
to predict how protein
complex is assembled,

00:27:17.779 --> 00:27:19.910
this is a molecular simulation
of the inside of bacterium.

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:21.990
And you can see that essentially,

00:27:21.990 --> 00:27:23.970
it's a very, very crowded place.

00:27:23.970 --> 00:27:27.580
All these proteins and
protein nucleic acid complexes

00:27:27.580 --> 00:27:30.801
are multi molecular complexes

00:27:30.801 --> 00:27:32.627
that are rubbing up against each other

00:27:32.627 --> 00:27:35.300
in a sort of gel like very,
very compact environment

00:27:35.300 --> 00:27:38.909
where there's hardly, there
are hardly any water molecules

00:27:38.909 --> 00:27:42.190
between them, although they
are in a aqueous solution.

00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:43.900
And so understanding

00:27:43.900 --> 00:27:48.020
how these large multi-subunit
components, these globules

00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:50.670
that you can see in the
interior of the cell assemble

00:27:51.629 --> 00:27:53.790
and how they find their partners

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:57.444
is a really fundamental
question in biology

00:27:57.444 --> 00:28:00.582
that kind of goes beyond
those individual components

00:28:00.582 --> 00:28:03.651
that are predicted by
AlphaFold to the next level

00:28:03.651 --> 00:28:05.525
of these molecular assemblies.

00:28:05.525 --> 00:28:09.240
And that's an area that I
worked on for over a dozen years

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:12.860
and where we're using the big
data for molecular structure.

00:28:12.860 --> 00:28:16.068
So the, what we're
looking at are proteins,

00:28:16.068 --> 00:28:17.822
the amino acid sequences,

00:28:17.822 --> 00:28:20.427
and they are really the output of genes.

00:28:20.427 --> 00:28:21.977
So genes are code level of DNA.

00:28:23.098 --> 00:28:25.727
They're transcribed into messenger RNA,

00:28:25.727 --> 00:28:27.527
which is the messenger molecule,

00:28:27.527 --> 00:28:29.367
the intermediate level of information

00:28:29.367 --> 00:28:30.865
that we'll come back onto later.

00:28:30.865 --> 00:28:32.980
And that's then translated
into the protein level.

00:28:32.980 --> 00:28:34.533
It's this amino acid level.

00:28:35.369 --> 00:28:37.502
And the question that
we're asking in this work

00:28:37.502 --> 00:28:38.670
is how do protein complexes assemble?

00:28:38.670 --> 00:28:41.430
And can we predict
these assembly pathways?

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:44.443
And so what we needed to
have in the first place

00:28:44.443 --> 00:28:48.890
is a big data set that we could,

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:51.510
we could build our models from.

00:28:51.510 --> 00:28:55.810
And that, we built that data set

00:28:55.810 --> 00:28:58.820
from over tens of thousands
of crystal structures

00:28:58.820 --> 00:29:00.420
that were available at the time.

00:29:01.289 --> 00:29:02.287
When we started this work,

00:29:02.287 --> 00:29:04.511
which was in the early 2000's actually,

00:29:04.511 --> 00:29:07.030
we published this really
complex database in 2004.

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:10.449
So we made our own database that provide

00:29:10.449 --> 00:29:12.820
where we modeled protein complexes

00:29:12.820 --> 00:29:15.061
that we first of all predicted

00:29:15.061 --> 00:29:16.470
to be in their physiological confirmation

00:29:16.470 --> 00:29:20.180
as graphs of the individual
amino acids sequences

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:23.200
where the edges that are
shown here between the graphs

00:29:24.282 --> 00:29:25.160
are the physical interfaces

00:29:25.160 --> 00:29:27.893
of the interacting protein subunits.

00:29:28.783 --> 00:29:31.450
And so this then allows
us to use graph theory

00:29:31.450 --> 00:29:35.410
as a theoretical basis
for relating complexes

00:29:35.410 --> 00:29:37.703
for matching them to each other,

00:29:37.703 --> 00:29:40.920
for finding sub components of these graphs

00:29:42.102 --> 00:29:43.660
that are connected to each other.

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:48.082
And if, what's important to
understand the basis of this

00:29:48.082 --> 00:29:51.230
is that over half of all protein complexes

00:29:51.230 --> 00:29:55.123
are assemblies of multiple
subunits of the same type.

00:29:55.123 --> 00:29:57.242
They're exactly they're called homomers.

00:29:57.242 --> 00:29:59.140
They're basically repeats
of the same subunit,

00:29:59.140 --> 00:30:01.640
and they can be related to each other

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:03.223
through axis of rotation.

00:30:07.933 --> 00:30:12.933
And they can be twofold,
as you can see here

00:30:13.195 --> 00:30:14.490
in a dihedral symmetry

00:30:15.793 --> 00:30:17.391
where there's a twofold axis of rotation,

00:30:17.391 --> 00:30:20.391
or they can be cyclic, which
can have any number of elements

00:30:22.340 --> 00:30:24.630
around the circle, sort
of in this donut shape.

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:28.233
We've got four here, but there
could be six, seven, eight

00:30:28.233 --> 00:30:29.493
and so on.

00:30:29.493 --> 00:30:31.311
And then all homermeric complexes,

00:30:31.311 --> 00:30:33.341
because they're closed symmetries,

00:30:33.341 --> 00:30:34.250
consist of combinations

00:30:34.250 --> 00:30:37.230
of these dihedral
two-fold axis of rotation

00:30:37.230 --> 00:30:40.100
and the larger cyclic axis of rotation.

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:42.900
And the important difference
between these is that in a,

00:30:43.999 --> 00:30:46.310
in this kind of flat donut-shaped

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:49.450
sort of repetitive structure
with a cyclic axis of rotation,

00:30:49.450 --> 00:30:52.318
you've got the head of one subunit

00:30:52.318 --> 00:30:53.895
connecting to the tail of the next,

00:30:53.895 --> 00:30:55.360
the head of one to tail
the next and so on.

00:30:55.360 --> 00:30:58.403
And so this interface consists
of two different surfaces.

00:30:59.301 --> 00:31:00.980
Whereas in the cyclic case,

00:31:00.980 --> 00:31:03.719
in this dihedral axis of
rotation, the two-fold access,

00:31:03.719 --> 00:31:06.730
it's actually the exact same interface.

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:09.454
It's two heads that are
contacting each other.

00:31:09.454 --> 00:31:11.480
It's exactly the same
surface that's mirrored,

00:31:11.480 --> 00:31:14.400
or that's used, not
mirrored, but that's used

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:17.050
within this dimeric protein structure.

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:19.050
And so that, that basically means

00:31:19.050 --> 00:31:21.080
that there's a different evolutionary,

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:23.250
there are different evolutionary
pathways and pressures

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:28.120
that make these different
symmetries of protein complexes.

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:31.923
And the very simplest, as
I said, to build something,

00:31:32.884 --> 00:31:35.287
either in evolution or
assembling inside a cell

00:31:35.287 --> 00:31:38.719
would be for a single protein
subunit to stick to itself.

00:31:38.719 --> 00:31:40.947
So it encounters another copy of itself,

00:31:40.947 --> 00:31:43.290
and it sticks to itself
through a dihedral axis,

00:31:43.290 --> 00:31:46.850
two-fold axis of rotation,
where it's like a handshake.

00:31:46.850 --> 00:31:50.067
It's like you shaking
your neighbor's hand.

00:31:50.067 --> 00:31:52.080
Your right hand, it's
the palm of your hand

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:54.440
is contacting the palm of their hand,

00:31:54.440 --> 00:31:57.423
so it's exactly the same
surface sticking to itself.

00:31:58.426 --> 00:31:59.628
It's two different,

00:31:59.628 --> 00:32:00.461
it's a hand shake kind of symmetry

00:32:00.461 --> 00:32:02.780
with a single two-fold axis of rotation.

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:04.190
And what that means in evolution

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:05.800
is that if there's one mutation,

00:32:05.800 --> 00:32:07.960
let's say in the fingers of your hand,

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:12.120
that increase the affinity or
stickiness for the other hand

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:15.870
in the hand shake, then that
mutation will come twice

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:19.280
and will increase the stickiness
of the two hands twice

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:21.650
because you have that symmetry.

00:32:21.650 --> 00:32:25.425
Now in the, in the
other, and here you can,

00:32:25.425 --> 00:32:27.788
you can have that process occurring again.

00:32:27.788 --> 00:32:30.772
And another set of
two-fold axis of rotation

00:32:30.772 --> 00:32:32.490
to make a dimer of dimers, in other words,

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:33.590
to form this tetramer.

00:32:34.690 --> 00:32:35.523
So once you've got the dimer,

00:32:35.523 --> 00:32:38.405
the two dimers can
interact with each other

00:32:38.405 --> 00:32:39.630
and form the tetrimer,

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:42.188
again with these interfaces

00:32:42.188 --> 00:32:44.190
that are reusing the same surface.

00:32:44.190 --> 00:32:45.760
And a different evolutionary

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:49.623
or even kinetic kind of
assembly scenario in the cell

00:32:49.623 --> 00:32:52.090
is that three subunits
encounter each other,

00:32:52.090 --> 00:32:55.925
and form a triangular here,

00:32:55.925 --> 00:32:58.940
interactions that are
occurring within the same plane

00:32:59.846 --> 00:33:01.730
with a single axis of
rotation down the middle in a,

00:33:01.730 --> 00:33:03.020
in a cyclic manner.

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:05.400
And here at what we need
is really, as I said,

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:07.740
for the head of one subunit to interact

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:11.151
with the bottom of the other sub unit,

00:33:11.151 --> 00:33:14.840
and for this to occur
for all three sub units,

00:33:14.840 --> 00:33:16.428
and what you,

00:33:16.428 --> 00:33:19.883
what one mutation would therefore
count three times within,

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:23.626
within, amongst those three subunits.

00:33:23.626 --> 00:33:26.026
And so you get a weak or
slower kind of increase

00:33:27.099 --> 00:33:28.210
of the affinity for the three sub units.

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:32.550
So one mutation would kind of
weakly increase the affinity

00:33:33.504 --> 00:33:35.697
across all three
interfaces in the same way.

00:33:35.697 --> 00:33:40.657
The same would go for four
subunits or five subunits.

00:33:40.657 --> 00:33:44.278
And this series can kind of
increase in the same way.

00:33:44.278 --> 00:33:46.919
So you see, you're kind of
building up these rings,

00:33:46.919 --> 00:33:48.960
and a single mutation
would slowly increase

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:51.662
the affinity across the whole ring,

00:33:51.662 --> 00:33:53.850
across all the different
interfaces at the same time.

00:33:53.850 --> 00:33:55.420
Now building up these,

00:33:55.420 --> 00:33:58.520
these stacks of rings can occur

00:33:58.520 --> 00:34:02.398
by either the hexamer over here

00:34:02.398 --> 00:34:04.050
with the dihedral three-fold symmetry

00:34:04.050 --> 00:34:06.850
could either form through a trimeric,

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:08.880
trimerization of the dimers.

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:11.130
So it can come through this three dimers

00:34:11.130 --> 00:34:12.280
sticking to each other.

00:34:13.297 --> 00:34:14.620
The octamer can be four dimers.

00:34:14.620 --> 00:34:17.081
The decamer can be five and so on.

00:34:17.081 --> 00:34:19.770
Or alternatively, we have this,

00:34:19.770 --> 00:34:22.290
these other pathways
where a trimer can stack

00:34:22.290 --> 00:34:25.000
to form a hexamer or a tetramer can stack

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:26.602
to form an octamer and so on.

00:34:26.602 --> 00:34:28.537
So you can get to these,

00:34:28.537 --> 00:34:29.719
there are two different paths

00:34:29.719 --> 00:34:30.690
to get to, for instance, the hexamer.

00:34:30.690 --> 00:34:33.170
One is from the monomer to
the dimer to the hexamer,

00:34:33.170 --> 00:34:35.374
the other one's from the monomer

00:34:35.374 --> 00:34:36.207
to the trimer to the hexamer,

00:34:36.207 --> 00:34:38.960
and these different intermediate states

00:34:39.820 --> 00:34:42.150
can be intermediates both in evolution

00:34:42.150 --> 00:34:46.084
or in the kinetic assembly, in a cell

00:34:46.084 --> 00:34:46.917
in a biochemical sense,

00:34:46.917 --> 00:34:48.900
and what we, that's what
we showed in this work.

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:52.678
And this was a paper that kind
of synthesizes these ideas,

00:34:52.678 --> 00:34:54.780
but that builds on
several other theoretical

00:34:54.780 --> 00:34:56.810
and computational analysis,

00:34:56.810 --> 00:34:59.930
and what we showed here
was the key principle

00:34:59.930 --> 00:35:03.380
that these pathways are
reflected in evolution

00:35:04.323 --> 00:35:05.270
in the sense that you find

00:35:06.622 --> 00:35:08.250
monomers related to dimers and tetramers,

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:11.790
but also trimers related
to dimers and hexamers,

00:35:11.790 --> 00:35:15.320
but all the pathways
that are not connected,

00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:18.080
for instance, a tetramer is never

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:21.710
or only very, very, very
rarely connected to a hexamer.

00:35:21.710 --> 00:35:25.799
A trimer is very, very rarely
connected to a tetramer

00:35:25.799 --> 00:35:26.800
and so on.

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:29.122
So the arrows in green and
red that you're seeing here

00:35:29.122 --> 00:35:31.761
are really the main
transitions that we see,

00:35:31.761 --> 00:35:33.750
and that holds both for
the evolutionary pathways

00:35:33.750 --> 00:35:36.082
and for assembly pathways.

00:35:36.082 --> 00:35:37.342
And we think that the reasons

00:35:37.342 --> 00:35:40.279
that these principles
of evolution assembly

00:35:40.279 --> 00:35:43.610
of protein complexes are
mirrored is that it's,

00:35:43.610 --> 00:35:47.698
it's the size of the interfaces
that really determine

00:35:47.698 --> 00:35:52.698
the intermediate forms that
are conserved in evolution.

00:35:52.730 --> 00:35:56.016
And also the intermediates
that are fastest,

00:35:56.016 --> 00:35:58.518
that are formed fastest kinetically.

00:35:58.518 --> 00:36:00.260
And so in a living cell.

00:36:00.260 --> 00:36:02.450
And so what that means
is that for instance,

00:36:02.450 --> 00:36:05.690
if you had the trimer as the
intermediate to the hexamer,

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:08.356
then these interfaces will be larger,

00:36:08.356 --> 00:36:09.792
would have a higher
affinity to each other,

00:36:09.792 --> 00:36:10.625
would be more stable,

00:36:10.625 --> 00:36:12.580
or if it were the dimer
that's the intermediate

00:36:12.580 --> 00:36:13.463
for the hexamer,

00:36:14.301 --> 00:36:17.280
it will be that dimeric
interface that's larger

00:36:17.280 --> 00:36:18.720
than the trimeric interface.

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:20.300
And that was really the
insight in this paper

00:36:20.300 --> 00:36:22.930
that comes through a large
data mining exercise.

00:36:22.930 --> 00:36:27.410
And we expanded this concept
for heteromeric complexes.

00:36:27.410 --> 00:36:29.530
These are complexes that consist

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:31.040
of subunits of different types.

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:34.430
So here you'd have an octamer
that consists of two subtypes

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:37.445
of the yellow subunits, two
of the red, two of the green,

00:36:37.445 --> 00:36:38.449
two of the blue.

00:36:38.449 --> 00:36:41.384
And if these, if these
assembled in a random manner,

00:36:41.384 --> 00:36:43.430
you know, you'd still be there
in the middle of the night,

00:36:43.430 --> 00:36:46.230
trying to find the correct
order for these jigsaw pieces

00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:47.340
to come together.

00:36:47.340 --> 00:36:49.130
Whereas if there's an ordered assembly

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:51.590
where the blue and the red
always form together first,

00:36:51.590 --> 00:36:53.160
and then the yellow, and then the green,

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:55.464
then you've got a sort
of click, click, click

00:36:55.464 --> 00:36:56.520
Ikea kind of assembly,

00:36:56.520 --> 00:36:58.540
and it's very rapid and efficient,

00:36:58.540 --> 00:37:02.767
and there are no miss
assemblies or aggregates

00:37:03.990 --> 00:37:06.650
that are sticky aggregates
that form erroneously

00:37:06.650 --> 00:37:07.830
inside the cell.

00:37:07.830 --> 00:37:10.170
And so it's really the
speed and the efficiency

00:37:10.170 --> 00:37:13.070
of that assembly that's driving that,

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:15.640
and we show that these
heteromeric assemblies

00:37:15.640 --> 00:37:20.640
also are reflected in the
evolutionary conservation.

00:37:21.380 --> 00:37:22.830
And in this case, what we're using

00:37:22.830 --> 00:37:25.310
is the principle of
gene fusion and fission

00:37:25.310 --> 00:37:29.010
because, of course, protein
subunits that are fused

00:37:29.010 --> 00:37:30.800
within the same gene

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:35.140
and that are part of the
same polypeptide chain

00:37:35.140 --> 00:37:36.650
will be covalently linked,

00:37:36.650 --> 00:37:41.030
and will be directly
therefore also kinetically

00:37:42.670 --> 00:37:46.110
the most efficient subunits
to form interfaces first.

00:37:46.110 --> 00:37:51.110
And indeed what we show is
that the genetic organization

00:37:51.570 --> 00:37:54.513
of protein subunits reflects
their assembly order.

00:37:55.510 --> 00:37:58.700
And then these predictions that are based

00:37:58.700 --> 00:38:01.389
on a combination of protein structure,

00:38:01.389 --> 00:38:03.000
they're based on gene structure

00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:06.940
in the two dimensional of
genome sequence of organisms,

00:38:06.940 --> 00:38:09.380
were verified experimentally

00:38:10.307 --> 00:38:11.869
through a beautiful collaboration

00:38:11.869 --> 00:38:13.471
that we had with Carol Robinson's group.

00:38:13.471 --> 00:38:14.720
Carl Robinson is a physical chemist,

00:38:14.720 --> 00:38:15.920
was one of the inventors

00:38:16.846 --> 00:38:18.365
of micro molecular mass spectrometry

00:38:18.365 --> 00:38:20.320
where you measure the mass
of intact protein complexes,

00:38:20.320 --> 00:38:22.010
and through collaboration with her

00:38:22.010 --> 00:38:27.010
and based on the protein, express proteins

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:31.253
from many generous collaborators
who gave the reagents,

00:38:32.912 --> 00:38:37.640
we showed that in vitro in
biochemical experiments,

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:41.810
our pathway predictions
were accurate kind of

00:38:42.690 --> 00:38:45.363
in the vast majority of cases.

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:54.190
And so overall in this body of work,

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:56.620
we're showing that
protein assembly pathways

00:38:56.620 --> 00:38:59.680
just like protein folding
itself is an order process.

00:38:59.680 --> 00:39:00.513
It's fast.

00:39:01.380 --> 00:39:02.220
It's spontaneous.

00:39:02.220 --> 00:39:04.970
It's predictable, and it's
also conserved in evolution.

00:39:05.813 --> 00:39:07.455
And these predictions are validated

00:39:07.455 --> 00:39:08.930
through these physical
chemistry experiments.

00:39:08.930 --> 00:39:12.160
And so you can think of the proteins,

00:39:12.160 --> 00:39:15.700
the amino acid sequence doesn't
only encode the structure.

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:18.057
It also encodes assembly instructions.

00:39:18.057 --> 00:39:19.180
You can think of it as
a set of building blocks

00:39:19.180 --> 00:39:22.430
where we've got two building blocks,

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:25.550
and we've got, the instruction
is that the red interface

00:39:25.550 --> 00:39:27.210
always binds with the blue interface.

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:29.600
G is neutral and exposed to the solvent.

00:39:29.600 --> 00:39:31.340
And that set of simple instructions

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:34.340
for these building blocks would
encode this cross structure.

00:39:35.234 --> 00:39:37.010
We've got the red
connecting to four blues,

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:39.060
and it makes this structured.

00:39:39.060 --> 00:39:40.940
In a way protein complexes
are kind of like that.

00:39:40.940 --> 00:39:43.760
And we can encode those using graph theory

00:39:43.760 --> 00:39:45.123
and complexity theory.

00:39:47.198 --> 00:39:49.660
This was a fantastic collaboration
with Sebastian Ahnert,

00:39:49.660 --> 00:39:51.760
my collaborator in the physics department.

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:55.220
And we show here that we can
develop a sort of shorthand

00:39:55.220 --> 00:39:58.510
for what the individual
protein subunits are

00:39:58.510 --> 00:40:01.600
and what interfaces they will
form with their partners.

00:40:01.600 --> 00:40:03.363
So we've got gray and white here,

00:40:04.416 --> 00:40:09.416
A interacts with C interacts with D

00:40:10.630 --> 00:40:15.630
and that basically forms
this square structure.

00:40:15.718 --> 00:40:17.820
On the other hand, if
we've got gray and white,

00:40:17.820 --> 00:40:21.060
but C interacts with B, and A with A

00:40:21.060 --> 00:40:22.660
we'll get this linear structure.

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:25.980
And with equal numbers of building blocks

00:40:25.980 --> 00:40:29.463
that are the general rule in nature,

00:40:30.733 --> 00:40:34.650
you take those principles into account,

00:40:34.650 --> 00:40:38.210
and you end up calculating
what we describe

00:40:38.210 --> 00:40:41.280
as a periodic table of protein complexes,

00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:45.416
and the power of the periodic
table of the elements

00:40:45.416 --> 00:40:47.948
just is similar in a way
to this periodic table

00:40:47.948 --> 00:40:50.220
of protein complexes
because the principles

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:52.810
of atoms and electrons in the
case of the periodic table

00:40:52.810 --> 00:40:54.683
predict features of the elements.

00:40:55.705 --> 00:40:58.310
Here are principles of
evolution and assembly

00:40:58.310 --> 00:41:01.250
of something that's predict structures

00:41:01.250 --> 00:41:03.990
that are of the individual elements here

00:41:03.990 --> 00:41:05.530
in terms of the number of repeats

00:41:05.530 --> 00:41:09.120
and the number of subunits
of cells in this matrix

00:41:09.120 --> 00:41:13.022
that are not, that were not
filled in by experiments,

00:41:13.022 --> 00:41:15.422
but that we are able to
fill in computationally.

00:41:16.261 --> 00:41:18.130
And those predictions
then were also verified

00:41:18.130 --> 00:41:21.473
by later releases of
the protein data bank.

00:41:23.020 --> 00:41:24.973
We also showed why,

00:41:31.145 --> 00:41:33.550
how this is kind of accommodated

00:41:33.550 --> 00:41:35.590
in the translation of proteins.

00:41:35.590 --> 00:41:38.940
And what you're seeing here is
a simulation of two elements

00:41:38.940 --> 00:41:39.860
of the ribosome.

00:41:39.860 --> 00:41:42.430
This is from Adrian Alcock
from IO in collaboration

00:41:43.277 --> 00:41:45.027
with my group, and this simulation,

00:41:46.505 --> 00:41:47.740
this molecular simulation
shows two polypeptide chains

00:41:48.757 --> 00:41:50.540
coming out of two adjacent ribosomes

00:41:50.540 --> 00:41:51.377
sitting on a messenger RNA.

00:41:51.377 --> 00:41:55.290
And what that tells us
is that the end terminus

00:41:55.290 --> 00:41:57.837
that comes out of the ribosomes first,

00:41:57.837 --> 00:42:01.980
so that's beginning of the
chains is likely to also interact

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:06.060
with it is likely to also interact first.

00:42:06.060 --> 00:42:11.060
And that kind of, that
sort of in vivo, you know,

00:42:13.270 --> 00:42:16.390
taking into account these in vivo kinetics

00:42:17.357 --> 00:42:21.050
of co-translational assembly also gives us

00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:24.460
much more detailed information
about the constraints

00:42:24.460 --> 00:42:27.098
on making homomeric proteins,

00:42:27.098 --> 00:42:30.280
and that the parts of
the amino acid chains

00:42:31.360 --> 00:42:34.740
that come out first are
interacting with each other

00:42:34.740 --> 00:42:36.870
before the other parts assemble.

00:42:36.870 --> 00:42:39.870
And so that's a kind of more
detailed level of the principle

00:42:39.870 --> 00:42:44.330
of how these polypeptide chains assemble

00:42:44.330 --> 00:42:46.700
in terms of homomeric relationships

00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:50.200
where exactly the same copies
of exactly the same protein

00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:52.910
are interacting with themselves,

00:42:52.910 --> 00:42:54.493
with more copies of themselves.

00:42:55.650 --> 00:42:58.250
So in summary, we've used
evolutionary relationships

00:42:58.250 --> 00:43:00.800
and principles of protein biophysics

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:02.600
to predict assembly pathways.

00:43:02.600 --> 00:43:06.130
They're inferences from
thousands of proteins structures

00:43:06.130 --> 00:43:10.286
and evolutionary sequence
relationships between proteins

00:43:10.286 --> 00:43:11.440
as well as gene structure.

00:43:11.440 --> 00:43:14.970
And our predictions were
experimentally validated

00:43:14.970 --> 00:43:18.276
both by macromolecular mass spectrometry

00:43:18.276 --> 00:43:22.276
and also by structures of proteins.

00:43:22.276 --> 00:43:23.270
And so that's really the lesson

00:43:23.270 --> 00:43:27.454
about predicting protein
complex assembly pathways.

00:43:27.454 --> 00:43:29.021
And you can see from this little story

00:43:29.021 --> 00:43:32.638
that it was a synergistic
exercise basically

00:43:32.638 --> 00:43:36.020
that involved bioinformatics
at the structural level,

00:43:36.020 --> 00:43:38.193
bioinformatics at the sequence level,

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:41.720
simulations like the molecular simulation

00:43:41.720 --> 00:43:45.771
that you just saw of the
ribosome making these proteins

00:43:45.771 --> 00:43:48.450
and coming out of the ribosomes,

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:53.450
coupled with physical chemistry,

00:43:53.896 --> 00:43:56.833
macromolecular mass
spectrometry experiments,

00:43:56.833 --> 00:43:59.683
and then also by physical
experiments that I didn't show.

00:44:01.030 --> 00:44:02.930
So there's really, you know, this science

00:44:02.930 --> 00:44:07.170
is really a collaborative effort

00:44:07.170 --> 00:44:10.250
between different disciplines

00:44:10.250 --> 00:44:12.980
and different types of
scientists working together

00:44:14.028 --> 00:44:18.040
to try to discover the truth centrally.

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:20.286
In the second part of my talk,

00:44:20.286 --> 00:44:22.040
I'm going to shift gears and go on

00:44:22.950 --> 00:44:26.653
to predicting a completely
different building block of life,

00:44:26.653 --> 00:44:30.043
and that is the cell, and the, at the,

00:44:31.634 --> 00:44:35.350
the basis of this is really
the evolution of genomics

00:44:35.350 --> 00:44:38.700
from sequencing DNA as in
the human genome project

00:44:38.700 --> 00:44:43.350
to sequencing RNA, which gives
us a molecular fingerprint

00:44:43.350 --> 00:44:47.191
of cells in terms of the subset of RNA

00:44:47.191 --> 00:44:49.625
that's inside each single cell.

00:44:49.625 --> 00:44:50.820
And it's that subset of messenger RNA

00:44:50.820 --> 00:44:54.120
that tells us about the
molecular features of the cell

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:58.296
and in terms of what proteins
would also be expressed

00:44:58.296 --> 00:44:59.550
inside that cell.

00:44:59.550 --> 00:45:00.760
And of course the cell is basically

00:45:00.760 --> 00:45:04.167
the fundamental unit of life.

00:45:04.167 --> 00:45:05.603
It's a component of the tissues,

00:45:06.493 --> 00:45:08.550
and the tissues in our body

00:45:08.550 --> 00:45:11.973
are the individual
micro-environments of organs.

00:45:13.453 --> 00:45:16.153
So the nose here, upper
respiratory system, the lungs,

00:45:17.149 --> 00:45:18.570
and lower respiratory system, the thymus,

00:45:18.570 --> 00:45:21.748
which is where T-cells are
made, the heart, and so on.

00:45:21.748 --> 00:45:24.433
Each one consists of many
different types of cells,

00:45:26.270 --> 00:45:29.030
and conventional RNA sequencing,

00:45:29.030 --> 00:45:31.490
sort of conventional bulk genomics

00:45:31.490 --> 00:45:34.775
used to require thousands
and thousands of cells

00:45:34.775 --> 00:45:38.320
to be mashed up together
before that the nucleic acid

00:45:39.210 --> 00:45:40.783
was extracted and put on the sequencer.

00:45:41.735 --> 00:45:46.560
Over the past, over almost 15 years,

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:49.730
the genomics has undergone

00:45:50.617 --> 00:45:52.834
a so-called resolution revolution,

00:45:52.834 --> 00:45:55.120
where we're now able to sequence
the nucleic acid content

00:45:56.004 --> 00:45:57.310
from each individual cell in a sample.

00:45:57.310 --> 00:45:59.293
And that's called single cell genomics.

00:46:00.212 --> 00:46:03.920
And it's that has really
opened up the ability

00:46:03.920 --> 00:46:06.800
to interrogate single cells
almost more powerfully

00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:08.193
than using a microscope.

00:46:09.332 --> 00:46:11.950
Because what we can do is
isolate individual cells,

00:46:11.950 --> 00:46:14.370
either in well plates or
using microfluidic robotics,

00:46:14.370 --> 00:46:19.370
and then extract or
label the messenger RNA,

00:46:20.889 --> 00:46:24.535
the comprehensive nucleic acid
content of each single cell

00:46:24.535 --> 00:46:27.868
and sequence that, and then
analyze the vast data sets

00:46:27.868 --> 00:46:30.480
that tell us the genes that
are active in single cells.

00:46:30.480 --> 00:46:34.392
And this has been a series
of technological innovations

00:46:34.392 --> 00:46:36.490
that's gone on from 2009 to date really

00:46:36.490 --> 00:46:39.370
with many different types
of isolation technologies,

00:46:39.370 --> 00:46:42.890
genomics protocols and
computational methods

00:46:42.890 --> 00:46:44.353
that have evolved at pace.

00:46:44.353 --> 00:46:47.868
And that now individual
experiments routinely encompass

00:46:47.868 --> 00:46:49.671
on the order of a million cells.

00:46:49.671 --> 00:46:50.860
And of course, that advance in technology

00:46:50.860 --> 00:46:55.493
has been, you know, absolutely
revolutionary for biology.

00:46:55.493 --> 00:47:00.493
I think it's not kind of an
underestimate to say that.

00:47:00.590 --> 00:47:05.590
And it's also been coupled
to kind of slightly behind

00:47:05.681 --> 00:47:08.728
the single cell genomics with
a spatial genomics revolution,

00:47:08.728 --> 00:47:09.980
which allows us to measure
nucleic acid content

00:47:09.980 --> 00:47:12.520
of tissue sections, where
the cells are actually

00:47:12.520 --> 00:47:14.929
in their native tissue context.

00:47:14.929 --> 00:47:17.088
So you're then taking a slice of a tissue,

00:47:17.088 --> 00:47:19.477
like a mouse brain that's shown here,

00:47:19.477 --> 00:47:22.020
and what we see here is the
expression of six genes,

00:47:22.020 --> 00:47:24.350
each in a different spatial region.

00:47:24.350 --> 00:47:26.240
And this is from members
of my group together

00:47:26.240 --> 00:47:29.253
with Omer Bayraktar's group
and Oliver Stegle's group

00:47:29.253 --> 00:47:30.910
that have developed

00:47:30.910 --> 00:47:33.370
a statistical probabilistic
inference framework

00:47:33.370 --> 00:47:37.855
for mapping individual cells
to spatial genomics data.

00:47:37.855 --> 00:47:41.570
And from the single cell genomics data,

00:47:41.570 --> 00:47:44.350
one of the key kind of
data mining exercise

00:47:44.350 --> 00:47:46.480
is to enforce cell types.

00:47:46.480 --> 00:47:50.330
And the way we do that as
using a set of algorithms

00:47:50.330 --> 00:47:52.763
called clustering, which was, you know,

00:47:53.760 --> 00:47:55.670
encompasses a vast array
of different approaches,

00:47:55.670 --> 00:47:59.340
but essentially what the exercise entails

00:47:59.340 --> 00:48:01.530
is finding individual data points

00:48:01.530 --> 00:48:04.877
that are similar to each
other and then grouping them.

00:48:04.877 --> 00:48:09.010
And what you're seeing here are data sets

00:48:09.010 --> 00:48:11.620
from gut, embryonic and fetal on the left,

00:48:11.620 --> 00:48:12.883
pediatric on the right.

00:48:13.759 --> 00:48:15.130
So they're intestinal samples,

00:48:15.130 --> 00:48:19.490
and each little spot on this
two-dimensional projection

00:48:19.490 --> 00:48:21.580
consists of a single cell.

00:48:21.580 --> 00:48:24.680
And that data point
encompasses in and of itself

00:48:24.680 --> 00:48:28.314
thousands and thousands
of gene expression levels.

00:48:28.314 --> 00:48:31.800
So it's a vast matrix of
hundreds of thousands of cells,

00:48:31.800 --> 00:48:34.810
each with thousands of
thousands of genes expressed.

00:48:34.810 --> 00:48:37.175
And the total number of genes, of course,

00:48:37.175 --> 00:48:40.010
in our human genome is
somewhere between 25 and 30,000,

00:48:40.010 --> 00:48:42.175
depending on how you count.

00:48:42.175 --> 00:48:43.750
And so this matrix that we're clustering

00:48:43.750 --> 00:48:46.480
consists of hundreds of thousands of cells

00:48:46.480 --> 00:48:51.333
multiplied, you know,
across 25,000 genes roughly.

00:48:52.321 --> 00:48:54.957
And then the exercise is to
cluster those data points

00:48:54.957 --> 00:48:58.434
that are similar with each other, and are,

00:48:58.434 --> 00:49:01.360
and that's basically
then our interpretation

00:49:01.360 --> 00:49:04.940
of the cell types that are
present in the data set.

00:49:04.940 --> 00:49:07.970
And you can see here that
interpretation's a combination

00:49:07.970 --> 00:49:10.137
of clustering and data mining,

00:49:10.137 --> 00:49:14.020
but also kind of like Priya
was asking at the beginning,

00:49:14.020 --> 00:49:15.222
there is intellectual input.

00:49:15.222 --> 00:49:16.233
There are hypotheses.

00:49:16.233 --> 00:49:17.066
There is external data

00:49:17.066 --> 00:49:19.000
that we're putting into
the interpretation.

00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:23.235
And they're, the cell types
are shown in different colors

00:49:23.235 --> 00:49:24.183
and annotated and different things.

00:49:25.038 --> 00:49:26.880
So you see immune cells,
they're kind of lumped together,

00:49:26.880 --> 00:49:28.920
neurons of the gut.

00:49:28.920 --> 00:49:32.010
We have a kind of brain
that surrounds our gut,

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:34.060
interestingly, they're labeled in yellow.

00:49:35.108 --> 00:49:39.450
We've got smooth muscle
that's around the gut

00:49:39.450 --> 00:49:41.350
and that's responsible for
sort of moving material

00:49:41.350 --> 00:49:42.830
through the gut, and
that's shown in brown,

00:49:42.830 --> 00:49:45.670
and the actual and enterocytes

00:49:45.670 --> 00:49:48.610
that are absorbing material inside the gut

00:49:48.610 --> 00:49:53.490
are all shown in the blue colors here.

00:49:53.490 --> 00:49:55.369
And so there are different,

00:49:55.369 --> 00:49:56.768
there are different cell types

00:49:56.768 --> 00:49:57.772
that are shown in different colors

00:49:57.772 --> 00:50:00.350
for the different clusters
based on the similarity

00:50:00.350 --> 00:50:03.120
in their expression profiles.

00:50:03.120 --> 00:50:06.108
And so, again, how the
cell clustering works

00:50:06.108 --> 00:50:07.310
is that we're grouping cells

00:50:07.310 --> 00:50:09.290
with similar expression patterns.

00:50:09.290 --> 00:50:12.469
In a way it's a hypothesis free grouping.

00:50:12.469 --> 00:50:14.600
It's a data mining
grouping on the one hand.

00:50:14.600 --> 00:50:16.766
On the other hand, as I said,

00:50:16.766 --> 00:50:19.965
there is in the final
interpretation of the data,

00:50:19.965 --> 00:50:22.589
there's often external
information that enters it.

00:50:22.589 --> 00:50:24.050
So there's a kind of intellectual puzzle

00:50:24.050 --> 00:50:26.730
where the scientist is
also putting in data.

00:50:26.730 --> 00:50:30.333
This is high dimensional data.

00:50:30.333 --> 00:50:31.801
Different clustering algorithms

00:50:31.801 --> 00:50:33.740
can indeed produce different results.

00:50:33.740 --> 00:50:36.020
So how do we know what result to use

00:50:36.020 --> 00:50:37.520
and what's the best algorithm?

00:50:38.390 --> 00:50:40.130
One of the tests for this is theoretical,

00:50:40.130 --> 00:50:41.820
and that is self consistency.

00:50:41.820 --> 00:50:44.892
And our single cell clustering
assessment framework

00:50:44.892 --> 00:50:45.910
is attempting to do exactly that.

00:50:45.910 --> 00:50:47.910
Sorry, this should be two C's and one A.

00:50:48.990 --> 00:50:52.720
That was a slight error here, so.

00:50:52.720 --> 00:50:55.200
And what we're doing here
is a cross validation,

00:50:55.200 --> 00:51:00.090
so a simulation or assessment
of the clustering results

00:51:00.090 --> 00:51:04.720
by testing whether the two
clusters can actually model

00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:08.440
and discover each other with the,

00:51:08.440 --> 00:51:10.973
using logistical question
machine learning method,

00:51:11.873 --> 00:51:13.530
or whether they are sufficiently distinct

00:51:13.530 --> 00:51:15.720
that they don't cross match to each other

00:51:16.627 --> 00:51:19.050
when we model their properties

00:51:19.050 --> 00:51:22.273
in terms of their weighted
levels of gene expression.

00:51:23.565 --> 00:51:26.550
And so there's basically ways

00:51:26.550 --> 00:51:28.950
of assessing the different
clustering algorithms

00:51:29.805 --> 00:51:31.610
from a theoretical and
computational point of view.

00:51:31.610 --> 00:51:35.080
And then there's of course
also ways of assessing

00:51:35.080 --> 00:51:38.740
the results from clustering
algorithms experimentally

00:51:39.627 --> 00:51:41.623
or using external data
from the literature.

00:51:42.565 --> 00:51:47.565
In any case, so the, we've
predicted cell types here

00:51:49.123 --> 00:51:51.377
for 20 tissues in the human body.

00:51:51.377 --> 00:51:53.230
This is almost a million
data points very recently.

00:51:53.230 --> 00:51:54.870
It's very exciting, you know,

00:51:54.870 --> 00:51:58.330
to be able to be at this
juncture where we have data

00:51:58.330 --> 00:52:01.460
that covers a representative
set of tissues

00:52:01.460 --> 00:52:03.190
from the whole body.

00:52:03.190 --> 00:52:04.484
It's not the entire body,

00:52:04.484 --> 00:52:06.724
but it's maybe half of
the tissues in our body.

00:52:06.724 --> 00:52:07.883
You know, we're getting very close,

00:52:08.786 --> 00:52:11.943
and it's a very exciting
time now for the community.

00:52:11.943 --> 00:52:16.110
And as I said, this isn't an automatic.

00:52:16.110 --> 00:52:18.558
You know, a million
cells, it's not something

00:52:18.558 --> 00:52:19.391
that you can do by hand.

00:52:19.391 --> 00:52:21.210
Gregor Mendel or Watson Crick, you know,

00:52:21.210 --> 00:52:24.244
those kinds of approaches
are not gonna work.

00:52:24.244 --> 00:52:26.160
We need to do this by machine learning.

00:52:26.160 --> 00:52:28.363
And we use a supervised or
semi-supervised approach

00:52:28.363 --> 00:52:31.290
so that we can then make
these models of cell types,

00:52:31.290 --> 00:52:35.100
which allows to transfer
labels from the known data sets

00:52:35.100 --> 00:52:36.710
to new datasets.

00:52:36.710 --> 00:52:40.022
And in general, of course,
you know, we're at the point

00:52:40.022 --> 00:52:44.740
where we're somewhere in
between these regimes.

00:52:44.740 --> 00:52:47.620
When you're, when you have little data,

00:52:47.620 --> 00:52:49.590
you require a lot of knowledge,

00:52:49.590 --> 00:52:51.941
and the more data that you have,

00:52:51.941 --> 00:52:53.760
the less knowledge you
really need to interpret it

00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:57.601
because you're kind of crystallizing.

00:52:57.601 --> 00:53:00.310
You have enough data sort of crystallized

00:53:01.164 --> 00:53:05.750
very, very accurately,
basically the, for instance,

00:53:05.750 --> 00:53:07.486
the clusters in your data sets,

00:53:07.486 --> 00:53:11.890
or the protein structures in
your AlphaFold predictions.

00:53:11.890 --> 00:53:13.780
And this is from Carl Henrik Ek

00:53:13.780 --> 00:53:17.010
who's a colleague of Neil
Henderson, Neil Lawrence

00:53:17.010 --> 00:53:20.972
in the computer science
department in Cambridge.

00:53:20.972 --> 00:53:23.760
And I think it summarizes
that the kind of development

00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:27.550
of single cell genomics
and computational biology

00:53:27.550 --> 00:53:30.703
in this area of cell type
annotation really nicely.

00:53:32.111 --> 00:53:34.189
So we are, we're getting to the era

00:53:34.189 --> 00:53:36.340
of large-scale single
cell expression data sets,

00:53:36.340 --> 00:53:39.940
where we can, we don't need
so much model selection

00:53:39.940 --> 00:53:41.113
and tuning anymore.

00:53:42.433 --> 00:53:46.420
And so the motivation is
where we can make classifiers

00:53:49.597 --> 00:53:52.550
that can automate the annotation.

00:53:52.550 --> 00:53:54.920
We want, we compiled this data

00:53:55.813 --> 00:53:57.311
from many, many data sets,

00:53:57.311 --> 00:54:00.760
19 different data sets from
different regions of the body

00:54:00.760 --> 00:54:03.420
and ask whether they're
tissue-specific cell states,

00:54:03.420 --> 00:54:05.810
whether they're cell states
that are shared across tissues.

00:54:05.810 --> 00:54:08.430
There are many really exciting questions

00:54:08.430 --> 00:54:11.000
that we can now use this data set for

00:54:11.000 --> 00:54:15.763
to interrogate the cells
from across the human body.

00:54:16.856 --> 00:54:21.856
We made, so they cluster into roughly

00:54:21.890 --> 00:54:24.350
100 different cell types.

00:54:24.350 --> 00:54:27.096
And there are subdivisions
of 10 broad cell types.

00:54:27.096 --> 00:54:29.293
And just to sort of give you
an overview of the workflow,

00:54:29.293 --> 00:54:30.220
you have to basically
integrate the datasets,

00:54:30.220 --> 00:54:32.593
harmonize, assembles, or clean the data,

00:54:33.534 --> 00:54:36.383
do initial training, model the data,

00:54:37.255 --> 00:54:42.255
and then you can feed
into unannotated new data

00:54:43.150 --> 00:54:45.990
and interrogate it with
the reference model.

00:54:45.990 --> 00:54:50.030
And that can in turn
then reenter the pipeline

00:54:50.030 --> 00:54:54.393
and contribute to the
training data in itself.

00:54:54.393 --> 00:54:55.920
And so you can go through
a kind of iterative process

00:54:55.920 --> 00:54:57.320
where you expand your models

00:54:57.320 --> 00:55:00.233
with more and more data systematically.

00:55:01.171 --> 00:55:02.832
And so basically the take home from this

00:55:02.832 --> 00:55:04.909
is that we're entering the area

00:55:04.909 --> 00:55:05.930
of big data in single cell genomics,

00:55:05.930 --> 00:55:09.374
just the way we did in protein structures

00:55:09.374 --> 00:55:12.392
in the previous section of
the talker on the AlphaFold

00:55:12.392 --> 00:55:14.593
and the biological
interpretation of that data

00:55:14.593 --> 00:55:16.351
just needs computational inference

00:55:16.351 --> 00:55:17.510
because it's just so vast.

00:55:17.510 --> 00:55:20.659
And the computational tools in mathematics

00:55:20.659 --> 00:55:21.870
on the other hand need to keep pace

00:55:21.870 --> 00:55:23.380
with the experimental technology.

00:55:23.380 --> 00:55:26.300
So there's a kind of to
and fro and a symbiosis

00:55:26.300 --> 00:55:29.023
between the theory and the computation.

00:55:29.950 --> 00:55:32.530
I'm coming to the last
part of the talk now,

00:55:32.530 --> 00:55:36.390
and where I'm going to discuss prediction

00:55:36.390 --> 00:55:39.190
of cell communication, how
cells talk to each other,

00:55:39.190 --> 00:55:42.030
how they communicate with each
other and how we can predict

00:55:42.030 --> 00:55:44.953
which cells get infected
using Human Cell Atlas data.

00:55:46.869 --> 00:55:49.712
So the Human Cell Atlas is
an international consortium

00:55:49.712 --> 00:55:52.354
with a mission of creating a
comprehensive reference map

00:55:52.354 --> 00:55:54.060
of cells using single cell genomics

00:55:54.060 --> 00:55:56.156
coupled with spatial data

00:55:56.156 --> 00:55:57.860
and interpreted with
computational methods.

00:55:57.860 --> 00:56:00.632
And it's, you can think
of it as a Google maps

00:56:00.632 --> 00:56:02.190
of the human body, where we're using

00:56:02.190 --> 00:56:03.610
this new cutting edge, high
resolution technologies

00:56:03.610 --> 00:56:06.770
to get from the kind of course
grain view of the human body

00:56:06.770 --> 00:56:08.920
to the Google street maps view.

00:56:08.920 --> 00:56:12.920
And we founded this about five years ago.

00:56:12.920 --> 00:56:15.273
This is this my co-founder,

00:56:16.268 --> 00:56:19.214
my partner in crime in this
endeavor is Aviv Regev.

00:56:19.214 --> 00:56:22.010
And we've now grown to a
community of about 2000 members

00:56:22.010 --> 00:56:25.060
across, you know, really across the globe,

00:56:25.060 --> 00:56:27.155
77 countries across the world.

00:56:27.155 --> 00:56:29.576
And this is a grassroots, bottom up,

00:56:29.576 --> 00:56:30.620
scientist-led initiative.

00:56:30.620 --> 00:56:33.590
We are organized into working
groups and biological networks

00:56:33.590 --> 00:56:36.550
that focus on the different
organs and tissues in the body,

00:56:36.550 --> 00:56:38.759
as well as human development,

00:56:38.759 --> 00:56:40.453
organoids and genetic diversity.

00:56:41.696 --> 00:56:42.653
And one of the first,

00:56:43.658 --> 00:56:47.440
my first project on
human cells and tissues

00:56:47.440 --> 00:56:50.930
as opposed to a mouse
or other model systems

00:56:50.930 --> 00:56:52.880
was the placenta.

00:56:52.880 --> 00:56:57.880
And what we set out to do
in from about 2015, 2016,

00:56:58.030 --> 00:57:00.240
was to map this organ.

00:57:00.240 --> 00:57:01.760
That's a transient organ.

00:57:01.760 --> 00:57:04.930
That's at the interface between
the mother and the fetus,

00:57:04.930 --> 00:57:07.240
sort of in the, in the inside of the womb.

00:57:07.240 --> 00:57:08.820
And it's of course only present

00:57:08.820 --> 00:57:10.650
for nine months of your life,

00:57:10.650 --> 00:57:12.870
but it's absolutely crucial and essential

00:57:12.870 --> 00:57:15.010
to your development for those nine months.

00:57:15.010 --> 00:57:17.970
And you wouldn't be
here without this organ.

00:57:17.970 --> 00:57:20.110
And it's relatively poorly understood

00:57:20.110 --> 00:57:24.790
because it's, the human is
very different from mouse

00:57:24.790 --> 00:57:25.980
and even different

00:57:25.980 --> 00:57:29.426
from the most closely
related non-human primates,

00:57:29.426 --> 00:57:30.259
the chimpanzees.

00:57:32.108 --> 00:57:33.258
It's shown here in red.

00:57:34.367 --> 00:57:38.670
And, of course, the really
intriguing conundrum to me here

00:57:38.670 --> 00:57:40.926
was that there's a mystery

00:57:40.926 --> 00:57:44.826
about how the maternal
immune system tolerates

00:57:44.826 --> 00:57:46.044
the paternal antigens.

00:57:46.044 --> 00:57:48.241
So of course our immune systems are tuned

00:57:48.241 --> 00:57:50.020
to reject anything that's non-self,

00:57:50.020 --> 00:57:52.240
and that's really the basis of our health

00:57:52.240 --> 00:57:53.640
and our homeostasis.

00:57:53.640 --> 00:57:56.710
And yet, in, when we're pregnant,

00:57:56.710 --> 00:58:00.686
we are tolerating
something that's non-self

00:58:00.686 --> 00:58:04.604
that has antigens or proteins
that come from the father.

00:58:04.604 --> 00:58:05.950
And so how is that actually possible?

00:58:05.950 --> 00:58:08.601
And what we set out to do therefore,

00:58:08.601 --> 00:58:11.557
was to study this tissue by dissociation,

00:58:11.557 --> 00:58:13.843
sorting cell, single cell genomics,

00:58:13.843 --> 00:58:15.741
using two different technologies,

00:58:15.741 --> 00:58:19.100
microfluidic droplets and well
plates studied robotically,

00:58:19.100 --> 00:58:22.191
and then computationally
cluster the cells.

00:58:22.191 --> 00:58:23.770
And you can see here, we
studied both the decidua,

00:58:23.770 --> 00:58:27.220
which is the uterus or
endometrium, the placenta,

00:58:27.220 --> 00:58:28.720
which is the, so the uterus
is the maternal side,

00:58:28.720 --> 00:58:29.817
the placenta is the fetal side,

00:58:29.817 --> 00:58:32.690
and the maternal blood in
order to distinguish cells

00:58:32.690 --> 00:58:35.653
that were from the maternal blood.

00:58:36.868 --> 00:58:40.589
And this gave us the cell
phone book, I'll call it,

00:58:40.589 --> 00:58:43.443
which means what are the
individual cellular components

00:58:43.443 --> 00:58:46.086
of both the maternal
side on the fetal side?

00:58:46.086 --> 00:58:47.868
And you can see here,
there are immune cells

00:58:47.868 --> 00:58:48.701
and K cells, T cells.

00:58:49.563 --> 00:58:51.368
There are glandular epithelial cells

00:58:51.368 --> 00:58:53.240
that do the secretions
to support the fetus.

00:58:53.240 --> 00:58:55.687
There are fibroblasts,

00:58:55.687 --> 00:58:58.420
which are kind of structural
components of the uterus.

00:58:58.420 --> 00:59:03.420
And it's really through
the statistical inference

00:59:04.106 --> 00:59:05.720
that we're able to find
all these cell types.

00:59:05.720 --> 00:59:07.330
But what we really wanted to understand

00:59:07.330 --> 00:59:10.043
was how does this immunological
tolerance take place?

00:59:11.047 --> 00:59:13.140
And to understand that, what
we need to also dig into

00:59:13.140 --> 00:59:15.802
is how the cells are
talking to each other.

00:59:15.802 --> 00:59:17.217
And we call this CellPhone,

00:59:17.217 --> 00:59:19.300
a statistical inference
system for deciphering

00:59:19.300 --> 00:59:23.824
cellular interactions through
the receptor ligand complex,

00:59:23.824 --> 00:59:26.622
and so this links to the
protein complex assembly

00:59:26.622 --> 00:59:28.150
that I talked about in
the first part of the talk

00:59:28.150 --> 00:59:32.090
and how those protein complexes

00:59:32.090 --> 00:59:34.400
on the surfaces of adjacent cells

00:59:34.400 --> 00:59:38.220
are mediating the
interactions between cells.

00:59:38.220 --> 00:59:40.870
So this is a kind of interaction

00:59:40.870 --> 00:59:42.560
that's at a higher
level than the proteins.

00:59:42.560 --> 00:59:44.830
It's at the level of the individual cells.

00:59:44.830 --> 00:59:47.717
We developed a statistical
inference framework

00:59:47.717 --> 00:59:50.400
to look for receptor/ligand
interactions between cell types,

00:59:50.400 --> 00:59:52.180
between single cell clusters

00:59:52.180 --> 00:59:54.070
that are specific to those cell types.

00:59:54.070 --> 00:59:56.015
So they're not ubiquitous.

00:59:56.015 --> 00:59:58.090
These aren't molecules that
are expressed everywhere.

00:59:58.090 --> 01:00:00.420
They're molecules that are
specifically complimentary

01:00:00.420 --> 01:00:02.820
between individual cell types.

01:00:02.820 --> 01:00:05.320
And that allowed us to decipher the cells.

01:00:05.320 --> 01:00:07.110
From the placental side, the fetal side

01:00:07.110 --> 01:00:09.138
that you can see up here

01:00:09.138 --> 01:00:11.100
where you've got the
placental trophoblast cells,

01:00:11.100 --> 01:00:13.819
you've got placental immune cells,

01:00:13.819 --> 01:00:14.652
blood vessels, fibroblasts.

01:00:14.652 --> 01:00:16.460
The maternal blood is
swishing around here.

01:00:17.340 --> 01:00:19.043
You've got extravillous trophoblast,

01:00:20.038 --> 01:00:22.040
which are the, invading the uterus,

01:00:22.040 --> 01:00:23.341
the maternal blood vessels,

01:00:23.341 --> 01:00:25.781
the maternal glands, and
maternal immune cells.

01:00:25.781 --> 01:00:28.310
And there are a lot of
interactions that are taking place

01:00:28.310 --> 01:00:31.661
that guarantee that
immunological tolerance

01:00:31.661 --> 01:00:32.494
that I mentioned earlier,

01:00:32.494 --> 01:00:33.983
and that we discovered in this work.

01:00:35.823 --> 01:00:38.621
And so this is really a kind of flavor

01:00:38.621 --> 01:00:40.282
of a Cell Atlas exercise.

01:00:40.282 --> 01:00:42.200
It's one of the first organs
that was mapped in this way.

01:00:42.200 --> 01:00:45.020
We mapped all different
regions of the placenta

01:00:45.020 --> 01:00:47.210
to get a comprehensive overview.

01:00:47.210 --> 01:00:52.210
And this, we published this work in 2018.

01:00:52.250 --> 01:00:55.137
And then of course over
the intervening years,

01:00:55.137 --> 01:00:59.190
there are a lot of
tissue and organ datasets

01:00:59.190 --> 01:01:00.580
that became available.

01:01:00.580 --> 01:01:04.477
And there are those 20 tissues
and almost a million cells

01:01:04.477 --> 01:01:06.260
that I mentioned early in
that integrated data set.

01:01:06.260 --> 01:01:08.920
And so when the pandemic
hit in early 2020,

01:01:08.920 --> 01:01:12.220
we became aware that there
was this virus circulating.

01:01:12.220 --> 01:01:15.695
What we leveraged was
the Human Cell Atlas data

01:01:15.695 --> 01:01:17.440
and also the Human Cell
Atlas scientific community

01:01:17.440 --> 01:01:19.017
to understand COVID-19

01:01:19.017 --> 01:01:21.017
and that endeavor has really continued.

01:01:21.017 --> 01:01:23.470
But I wanna tell you this
story from the very early days

01:01:23.470 --> 01:01:25.160
of the pandemic,

01:01:25.160 --> 01:01:26.790
where we mapped the viral entry receptors

01:01:26.790 --> 01:01:29.112
from the Human Cell Atlas data.

01:01:29.112 --> 01:01:31.499
So we asked in all of this
single cell genomics data

01:01:31.499 --> 01:01:32.332
from around the body,

01:01:32.332 --> 01:01:34.210
where are the viral
entry receptors expressed

01:01:34.210 --> 01:01:37.070
that could welcome the
virus into the cells?

01:01:37.070 --> 01:01:38.590
Because of course the virus is docking

01:01:38.590 --> 01:01:41.590
onto the surface of cells,
and so we're simply asking,

01:01:41.590 --> 01:01:44.810
can we predict where the virus is entering

01:01:44.810 --> 01:01:48.610
in the healthy Human Cell Atlas data?

01:01:48.610 --> 01:01:51.130
And we assembled data
from all around the body.

01:01:51.130 --> 01:01:53.200
You can see the different tissues here,

01:01:53.200 --> 01:01:55.373
and then mapped where ACE2 is expressed,

01:01:56.346 --> 01:01:59.387
and I'm gonna focus very
briefly on the barrier tissues,

01:01:59.387 --> 01:02:02.185
where the healthy reference
data is probably most useful

01:02:02.185 --> 01:02:04.269
and is telling us where the virus can hit.

01:02:04.269 --> 01:02:06.260
And of course, the nose
is one of the main ones

01:02:06.260 --> 01:02:09.770
where we have the nasal passages
where you've got inhalation

01:02:09.770 --> 01:02:11.670
of aerosol droplets.

01:02:11.670 --> 01:02:14.289
And indeed here, we find epithelial cells,

01:02:14.289 --> 01:02:17.226
goblet and ciliated cells that
have high expression levels

01:02:17.226 --> 01:02:18.608
for ACE2 and TMPRSS2.

01:02:18.608 --> 01:02:20.820
So we pointed towards those specific cells

01:02:20.820 --> 01:02:23.850
as being potential viral entry points.

01:02:23.850 --> 01:02:26.711
In the lower airways, the bronchi,

01:02:26.711 --> 01:02:28.006
you've got club and ciliated.

01:02:28.006 --> 01:02:31.070
Sorry, my internet disconnected.

01:02:31.070 --> 01:02:32.743
- [Priya] Yeah, no worries, yeah.

01:02:34.290 --> 01:02:36.470
- So we found cells in the eye

01:02:36.470 --> 01:02:41.470
and in the gut epithelium and teracytes

01:02:43.203 --> 01:02:44.660
and also in the placenta,

01:02:44.660 --> 01:02:47.710
which I just talked about
where we hypothesized

01:02:47.710 --> 01:02:50.010
that there are cells
that can be responsible

01:02:50.010 --> 01:02:52.003
for the vertical transmission

01:02:52.003 --> 01:02:53.983
from the maternal to the fetal side.

01:02:53.983 --> 01:02:56.503
Now, while this is rare,
what this data shows

01:02:56.503 --> 01:02:58.570
is that there's a potential
pathway of transmission.

01:02:58.570 --> 01:03:00.503
These were all predictions

01:03:00.503 --> 01:03:01.970
about where the virus could enter.

01:03:01.970 --> 01:03:04.200
We were trying to get
to truth and information

01:03:04.200 --> 01:03:08.700
and insights into this
infection as quickly as possible

01:03:08.700 --> 01:03:10.761
using Cell Atlas data.

01:03:10.761 --> 01:03:12.931
And what's really exciting
is that since then,

01:03:12.931 --> 01:03:13.764
we published that work last year,

01:03:13.764 --> 01:03:16.740
it's been cited about 1300 times.

01:03:16.740 --> 01:03:18.460
It got a lot of attention also

01:03:18.460 --> 01:03:21.090
from the public health point of view.

01:03:21.090 --> 01:03:23.210
What's really exciting
is that those predictions

01:03:23.210 --> 01:03:25.800
have largely come true
from experimental data

01:03:25.800 --> 01:03:28.580
where we can see, for
instance, from nasal swabs

01:03:28.580 --> 01:03:29.823
that we can read,

01:03:31.788 --> 01:03:35.120
next generation sequencing
reads mRNA of the virus

01:03:35.120 --> 01:03:38.510
inside individual cells
in the nasal epithelium.

01:03:38.510 --> 01:03:39.960
I should also say that in the mouth,

01:03:39.960 --> 01:03:44.960
which I haven't discussed,
we predict salivary glands,

01:03:45.545 --> 01:03:48.947
ductile cells in the top
of this salivary glands,

01:03:48.947 --> 01:03:50.470
in the ducts where the virus could enter.

01:03:50.470 --> 01:03:52.140
And indeed, in these microscopy images,

01:03:52.140 --> 01:03:54.526
we can see that the virus is indeed

01:03:54.526 --> 01:03:57.347
sitting inside those cells, and
we published that this year.

01:03:57.347 --> 01:03:59.653
So for ACES and the SARS-CoV-2 virus,

01:04:00.505 --> 01:04:01.910
the correlation with the receptor

01:04:01.910 --> 01:04:06.501
does indeed reflect the
infection and the infected cells.

01:04:06.501 --> 01:04:08.765
And so that's really the
last part of my story.

01:04:08.765 --> 01:04:10.490
I've told you about protein
complex assembly pathways

01:04:10.490 --> 01:04:12.563
and data mining from protein structures,

01:04:13.509 --> 01:04:14.750
inferences from single cell genomics

01:04:14.750 --> 01:04:17.023
to predict cell types and cell states,

01:04:17.862 --> 01:04:20.785
the clustering, predicting
cell communication

01:04:20.785 --> 01:04:21.618
of the maternal fetal interface,

01:04:21.618 --> 01:04:24.400
and viral entry into cells

01:04:24.400 --> 01:04:26.190
through the cell surface receptors.

01:04:26.190 --> 01:04:28.840
And so I'd like to summarize
basically at a high level,

01:04:28.840 --> 01:04:31.750
what this is telling us
about correlation, causation

01:04:31.750 --> 01:04:33.810
is that in the protein complex assembly,

01:04:33.810 --> 01:04:38.360
this data mining approaches
for linking evolution assembly,

01:04:38.360 --> 01:04:41.330
the correlation was
experimentally validated

01:04:41.330 --> 01:04:43.540
using physical chemistry,
mass spectrometry,

01:04:43.540 --> 01:04:44.843
and other methods.

01:04:45.690 --> 01:04:50.130
In single cell genomics,
the cell typist models

01:04:50.130 --> 01:04:54.220
really rely on, the
data is of such a scale

01:04:54.220 --> 01:04:56.100
that the interpretation really relies

01:04:56.100 --> 01:04:59.640
on machine learning and
computational tools like clustering.

01:04:59.640 --> 01:05:01.858
And for the Human Cell Atlas,

01:05:01.858 --> 01:05:04.460
What I told you about the cell
interactions in the placenta

01:05:04.460 --> 01:05:06.739
and the COVID-infected cells

01:05:06.739 --> 01:05:10.130
is that these correlations
are now coming true

01:05:10.130 --> 01:05:13.842
based on orthogonal
experimental measurements,

01:05:13.842 --> 01:05:14.720
and later experimental data.

01:05:14.720 --> 01:05:17.720
And with that, I'd like to thank
you and take any questions.

01:05:20.260 --> 01:05:21.760
- Thank you so much, Sarah.

01:05:21.760 --> 01:05:25.582
First of all for your enormous patience

01:05:25.582 --> 01:05:27.610
through the disruptions for today,

01:05:27.610 --> 01:05:30.910
what an exciting set of experiments

01:05:30.910 --> 01:05:33.770
and conceptual model building

01:05:33.770 --> 01:05:38.190
that you and collaborators
have been working on,

01:05:38.190 --> 01:05:40.838
and truly interdisciplinary.

01:05:40.838 --> 01:05:43.560
So thank you so much for
allowing us a glimpse

01:05:43.560 --> 01:05:47.800
into this world and the results.

01:05:47.800 --> 01:05:52.100
So I think we have a couple of questions.

01:05:52.100 --> 01:05:56.260
We had one from Deepti in the chat,

01:05:56.260 --> 01:05:58.010
and her question is

01:05:58.010 --> 01:06:00.943
how do you account for
heterogeneity of time?

01:06:02.198 --> 01:06:03.530
What is the time that you are using

01:06:03.530 --> 01:06:05.693
in the spatial clustering analysis?

01:06:08.270 --> 01:06:12.750
- So these are all snapshots essentially.

01:06:12.750 --> 01:06:17.750
So in the, yeah, so the
example in the spatial genomics

01:06:18.803 --> 01:06:19.800
of the mouse brain for instance,

01:06:19.800 --> 01:06:23.957
you know, that's a snapshot
for at one point in time

01:06:23.957 --> 01:06:26.999
through a tissue section,
same for the placenta.

01:06:26.999 --> 01:06:28.160
You know, it's a snapshot of a tissue

01:06:29.019 --> 01:06:30.240
that you're measuring
at one point in time.

01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:31.683
Now within that,

01:06:32.839 --> 01:06:35.440
there are kind of pseudo
temporal relationships

01:06:35.440 --> 01:06:38.130
between cells, which I didn't discuss

01:06:38.130 --> 01:06:42.860
because you have cells that
are progenitors of other cells.

01:06:42.860 --> 01:06:44.470
So you kind of have stem cells

01:06:44.470 --> 01:06:48.100
that give rise to other cells,
and those relationships,

01:06:48.100 --> 01:06:50.260
I mean, inferring those relationships

01:06:50.260 --> 01:06:53.200
is another whole field in and of itself.

01:06:53.200 --> 01:06:55.970
And actually in some of the
gut data that I mentioned,

01:06:55.970 --> 01:06:59.470
there are stem cells
and differentiated cells

01:06:59.470 --> 01:07:01.760
within the epithelial compartment,

01:07:01.760 --> 01:07:06.093
and in the data projection
that I showed in 2-D,

01:07:07.041 --> 01:07:08.780
you can almost see those relationships

01:07:08.780 --> 01:07:11.901
where the stem cells are
kind of more at the bottom,

01:07:11.901 --> 01:07:13.650
at the root of a trajectory,

01:07:13.650 --> 01:07:16.800
and the differentiated cells
are kind of more at the top,

01:07:16.800 --> 01:07:19.859
but we weren't formally
doing that inference here.

01:07:19.859 --> 01:07:23.433
It just comes out in the
manifold projection, yeah.

01:07:25.641 --> 01:07:26.790
- Thank you.

01:07:26.790 --> 01:07:28.920
If I may, I have a question.

01:07:28.920 --> 01:07:33.920
So, you know, one of the
arguments that is always made

01:07:34.000 --> 01:07:39.000
about, you know, comparing
disciplinary approaches,

01:07:39.180 --> 01:07:42.940
say physics and biology,
is that, you know,

01:07:42.940 --> 01:07:47.940
in physics we have some
guidance from conservation laws.

01:07:47.979 --> 01:07:49.180
So, you know, you have
conservation of energy,

01:07:49.180 --> 01:07:51.870
conservation of angular
momentum and so on.

01:07:51.870 --> 01:07:55.680
And those really form the bedrock

01:07:55.680 --> 01:07:58.919
on which we build inference models

01:07:58.919 --> 01:08:01.139
because these give you constraints.

01:08:01.139 --> 01:08:05.853
So, and the question
often arises, are there,

01:08:06.863 --> 01:08:08.540
when there are clearly symmetries,

01:08:08.540 --> 01:08:13.250
do these symmetries similarly
translate into conservation,

01:08:13.250 --> 01:08:18.250
new conservation laws or are
there just similar sort of,

01:08:18.298 --> 01:08:19.831
you know, entropy and energy?

01:08:19.831 --> 01:08:21.810
I mean, are these the
sorts of conservation laws

01:08:21.810 --> 01:08:22.703
that operate?

01:08:25.340 --> 01:08:28.799
- No, I mean, that's a great,
that's a great question.

01:08:28.799 --> 01:08:33.799
I'd say, so there are some, I mean,

01:08:34.840 --> 01:08:38.000
at one level biology is physics.

01:08:38.000 --> 01:08:41.474
And so, you know, in
biophysics, like in the,

01:08:41.474 --> 01:08:43.057
in the macromolecular assembly,

01:08:43.057 --> 01:08:45.180
you know, at some level, this is physics.

01:08:45.180 --> 01:08:47.998
It's biophysics, it's physical chemistry.

01:08:47.998 --> 01:08:50.818
The laws of physics apply,
and of course they apply

01:08:50.818 --> 01:08:52.540
to all of biology in that sense,

01:08:52.540 --> 01:08:56.490
but when you're dealing with
the sort of big data sets,

01:08:56.490 --> 01:08:59.390
then you're not in that regime,

01:08:59.390 --> 01:09:03.037
but there are still
like sort of, you know,

01:09:04.222 --> 01:09:06.242
the central dogma that I showed,

01:09:06.242 --> 01:09:08.092
which is DNA makes RNA makes protein.

01:09:09.080 --> 01:09:12.440
Like that central dogma would still enter

01:09:12.440 --> 01:09:16.220
into any of these data mining assumptions.

01:09:16.220 --> 01:09:20.360
So for instance, we are predicting
cell types from the RNA,

01:09:20.360 --> 01:09:24.550
but our kind of unarticulated
assumption is that those,

01:09:24.550 --> 01:09:27.280
those RNA fingerprints will translate into

01:09:27.280 --> 01:09:29.623
what proteins the cell is expressing.

01:09:30.518 --> 01:09:33.510
So there are some fundamental
truths that everybody has

01:09:33.510 --> 01:09:37.703
as part of their kind of
mental map when they're doing,

01:09:38.575 --> 01:09:41.217
you know, when they're
doing biological science.

01:09:41.217 --> 01:09:46.217
- But do they then become
constraints as well?

01:09:46.420 --> 01:09:48.176
So--

01:09:48.176 --> 01:09:50.133
- Can, they can, yes, they can.

01:09:57.583 --> 01:09:59.560
Yes, and, you know,

01:09:59.560 --> 01:10:02.068
because the central
dogma may not always hold

01:10:02.068 --> 01:10:03.973
because you may have RNA

01:10:04.988 --> 01:10:07.147
that doesn't make protein, you know,

01:10:07.147 --> 01:10:11.620
and so that can, you know,
that correlation may not hold

01:10:11.620 --> 01:10:13.653
or that assumption may be misleading,

01:10:14.746 --> 01:10:17.750
but I would say it's the
exception rather than the rule.

01:10:17.750 --> 01:10:18.583
- [Priya] Right.

01:10:19.542 --> 01:10:20.375
- ]Sarah] Yeah, but,
yeah, I get your point.

01:10:20.375 --> 01:10:23.239
- Right, because I mean,
I think that is sort of

01:10:23.239 --> 01:10:26.920
one of the features
when you make inferences

01:10:26.920 --> 01:10:31.777
in sort of physical systems,
and if you have, you know,

01:10:33.566 --> 01:10:36.480
you're looking for these
unmapped correlations

01:10:36.480 --> 01:10:40.400
and when you find them, you still,

01:10:40.400 --> 01:10:42.700
if some of them violate

01:10:42.700 --> 01:10:45.524
the conservation of energy, for example,

01:10:45.524 --> 01:10:49.560
then you know that you are
probably missing a variable

01:10:49.560 --> 01:10:52.970
or there is a hidden
variable that, you know,

01:10:52.970 --> 01:10:57.560
you in your inference
structure you have overlooked,

01:10:57.560 --> 01:10:59.630
or, and so on.

01:10:59.630 --> 01:11:01.810
So I guess what you're saying

01:11:01.810 --> 01:11:04.767
is that it's not quite the same.

01:11:04.767 --> 01:11:06.167
- Yeah, I mean there are,
so I see what you mean.

01:11:06.167 --> 01:11:10.090
So there are kind of like sense
tracks, you know, like that.

01:11:10.090 --> 01:11:14.320
And one of them would be, so
for instance, that, you know,

01:11:14.320 --> 01:11:16.690
cell types that are,

01:11:16.690 --> 01:11:18.780
that have developed from
different progenitors

01:11:18.780 --> 01:11:20.726
or that are completely different from,

01:11:20.726 --> 01:11:22.381
let's say epithelial and neuronal.

01:11:22.381 --> 01:11:24.140
So the, you know, the brain
and the epithelial tissues.

01:11:24.140 --> 01:11:26.060
Those cell types should never, let's say,

01:11:26.060 --> 01:11:28.000
logistical question models,
machine learning models

01:11:28.000 --> 01:11:30.529
should never cross match each other.

01:11:30.529 --> 01:11:32.680
And so that was kind of the
internal consistency check

01:11:32.680 --> 01:11:36.050
that I mentioned where
we're looking for, you know,

01:11:36.050 --> 01:11:38.930
set clusterings that are
separate, or that are,

01:11:38.930 --> 01:11:41.210
that are hitting each other,
that are finding each other.

01:11:41.210 --> 01:11:43.650
There's that kind of
thing that you can use.

01:11:43.650 --> 01:11:45.268
- [Priya] Right.

01:11:45.268 --> 01:11:47.508
- So that's based on a sort
of developmental, you know.

01:11:47.508 --> 01:11:48.785
- Right, so I guess there is--

01:11:48.785 --> 01:11:50.009
(cross talking between Sarah and Priya)

01:11:50.009 --> 01:11:52.260
So I guess the way this
would kind of connect

01:11:52.260 --> 01:11:54.300
to a physics kind of
argument that I was making

01:11:54.300 --> 01:11:58.250
is that one constraint
would be that, you know,

01:11:58.250 --> 01:12:01.370
these cells cannot
transmute into each other.

01:12:01.370 --> 01:12:03.030
That's a fundamental constraint.

01:12:03.030 --> 01:12:03.863
- Yeah.

01:12:04.748 --> 01:12:06.130
- That they have, they are
independent building blocks

01:12:06.130 --> 01:12:08.730
that don't transform into each other.

01:12:08.730 --> 01:12:10.551
- Exactly, exactly.

01:12:10.551 --> 01:12:12.190
- Okay, great.

01:12:12.190 --> 01:12:13.172
Thank you.

01:12:13.172 --> 01:12:14.005
Any other questions?

01:12:16.331 --> 01:12:17.164
- [Participant] I just
wanted to make a comment

01:12:17.164 --> 01:12:20.730
on what you said at
the last moment, Priya,

01:12:20.730 --> 01:12:23.420
about the cells can't become another cell.

01:12:23.420 --> 01:12:25.820
Well, as long as you're
not looking at stem cells,

01:12:25.820 --> 01:12:27.388
right, Sarah?

01:12:27.388 --> 01:12:28.532
- Right, right, yes.

01:12:28.532 --> 01:12:30.110
I mean, I guess they.

01:12:30.110 --> 01:12:31.420
- Right, but the progenitors, you know,

01:12:31.420 --> 01:12:35.760
let's say gut, you know, stem
cell wouldn't become a brain.

01:12:35.760 --> 01:12:38.448
I mean, naturally, you know,

01:12:38.448 --> 01:12:40.130
so the neuronal progenitors
will be different

01:12:40.130 --> 01:12:42.823
from the gut, epithelial progenitors.

01:12:44.690 --> 01:12:46.670
I mean, that's a kind
of reasonable assumption

01:12:46.670 --> 01:12:48.163
for any biologist I think.

01:12:49.030 --> 01:12:51.070
You can force them to do
those things in the dish

01:12:51.070 --> 01:12:53.412
in a Frankensteinian kind of way,

01:12:53.412 --> 01:12:55.183
but they wouldn't happen
naturally necessarily.

01:12:57.100 --> 01:13:00.370
- Right, so I think while
we wait for someone else,

01:13:00.370 --> 01:13:01.951
I mean, I have.

01:13:01.951 --> 01:13:03.814
- Well, I think Jenny
wants to ask a question.

01:13:03.814 --> 01:13:05.867
- [Participant] Yep,
Jenny has her hand up.

01:13:05.867 --> 01:13:06.908
- Oh, there's a hand up.

01:13:06.908 --> 01:13:08.412
Okay, sorry, please go ahead, Jenny.

01:13:08.412 --> 01:13:09.245
- [Jenny] Okay, thanks.

01:13:09.245 --> 01:13:10.109
I just try to be polite.

01:13:10.109 --> 01:13:12.250
Thanks for the great talk.

01:13:12.250 --> 01:13:14.143
Concerning Priya's question as well,

01:13:14.989 --> 01:13:16.497
I would like to follow up on this.

01:13:16.497 --> 01:13:20.660
You have single cells, and you
try to cluster them by type,

01:13:20.660 --> 01:13:23.020
but they are not, you
do not take into account

01:13:23.020 --> 01:13:24.880
the environment of these cells.

01:13:24.880 --> 01:13:26.830
This comes only when you take into account

01:13:26.830 --> 01:13:28.283
the Human Cell Atlas.

01:13:29.353 --> 01:13:32.300
So would your clustering types then change

01:13:32.300 --> 01:13:36.080
by taking into account the
environment of each cell type?

01:13:36.080 --> 01:13:38.137
Will you get different clusters,

01:13:38.137 --> 01:13:40.387
by taking this, the
environment into account?

01:13:42.140 --> 01:13:45.177
- I mean, so in theory,
you know, if the data,

01:13:45.177 --> 01:13:48.800
like you shouldn't.

01:13:48.800 --> 01:13:53.070
I mean, the molecular fingerprint
based on the RNA content

01:13:53.070 --> 01:13:57.330
should, you know, we think
that that should be sufficient

01:13:57.330 --> 01:14:00.763
to determine the identity of a cell.

01:14:01.777 --> 01:14:03.679
But of course, if you have
additional information

01:14:03.679 --> 01:14:06.334
about the environment, if
you have, let's say a cluster

01:14:06.334 --> 01:14:08.340
that's large and very disperse,

01:14:08.340 --> 01:14:13.340
and it, and, you know,
in the 20,000 gene space,

01:14:14.060 --> 01:14:18.780
then you may, you know,
having the precise micro,

01:14:18.780 --> 01:14:21.693
anatomical, micro environmental
niche information,

01:14:22.538 --> 01:14:24.888
the tissue, may tell you,
oh, you know, in the,

01:14:26.341 --> 01:14:29.883
in the crypts of the colon, there's this,

01:14:30.810 --> 01:14:33.660
let's say, intraepithelial T cell type,

01:14:33.660 --> 01:14:34.736
whereas in the villi,

01:14:34.736 --> 01:14:37.356
there's this intraepithelial T cell type.

01:14:37.356 --> 01:14:40.299
And we can now distinguish them, you know,

01:14:40.299 --> 01:14:42.170
based on very, very,
very subtle differences

01:14:42.170 --> 01:14:44.340
within this cluster

01:14:44.340 --> 01:14:46.440
because we have the micro
environmental information.

01:14:46.440 --> 01:14:49.059
I mean, that's what,
that's one of the things

01:14:49.059 --> 01:14:50.180
that we developed this,

01:14:50.180 --> 01:14:53.500
this method called Milo
with John Marioni's group

01:14:53.500 --> 01:14:54.760
that basically distinguishes

01:14:54.760 --> 01:14:57.238
sort of very subtle
neighborhoods in a KNN graph

01:14:57.238 --> 01:15:00.199
of the single cell genomics data

01:15:00.199 --> 01:15:01.600
based on external information,

01:15:01.600 --> 01:15:03.460
the kind of metadata parameters

01:15:03.460 --> 01:15:06.463
that you can give the algorithm.

01:15:07.920 --> 01:15:09.660
So, yeah, that's a, that's a good point.

01:15:09.660 --> 01:15:14.660
Taking into account
metadata about location

01:15:14.750 --> 01:15:17.093
can resolve clusters in a more,

01:15:18.570 --> 01:15:20.313
in a more fine-grained way.

01:15:21.640 --> 01:15:23.262
- [Jenny] Thanks.

01:15:23.262 --> 01:15:24.095
So you get sub clusters.

01:15:24.095 --> 01:15:28.980
- Yeah, and sub clusters
that are, that you couldn't,

01:15:28.980 --> 01:15:29.880
where there wouldn't be
sufficient information

01:15:29.880 --> 01:15:31.710
based on the single cell
genomics data alone,

01:15:31.710 --> 01:15:33.343
but using the metadata,

01:15:34.257 --> 01:15:37.070
you then have enough
statistical power and basis

01:15:38.088 --> 01:15:40.369
to distinguish them.

01:15:40.369 --> 01:15:42.506
- [Jenny] Mmm hmm, thanks.

01:15:42.506 --> 01:15:43.900
- [Priya] Wilson?

01:15:43.900 --> 01:15:45.480
- [Moderator] Hussein?

01:15:45.480 --> 01:15:49.007
- [Wilson] Yeah, I have a
question more about prediction

01:15:49.007 --> 01:15:50.208
towards the future.

01:15:50.208 --> 01:15:53.150
So I'm curious, as I know
that the Human Cell Atlas

01:15:53.150 --> 01:15:56.750
is in the process of building
the map of human cells.

01:15:56.750 --> 01:15:59.080
So just like we learned
about the differences

01:15:59.080 --> 01:16:01.880
between the sequences of
individual human genomes

01:16:02.730 --> 01:16:03.563
on the bulk level,

01:16:03.563 --> 01:16:06.230
where do you see this going

01:16:06.230 --> 01:16:08.200
in terms of this single cell level,

01:16:09.632 --> 01:16:10.730
in terms of different individuals having,

01:16:10.730 --> 01:16:14.143
I don't know, different
numbers of cells or cell types

01:16:14.143 --> 01:16:15.944
or expression profiles and so forth.

01:16:15.944 --> 01:16:19.067
- Yeah, so the human genetics
level for the Human Cell Atlas

01:16:19.067 --> 01:16:23.071
is basically coming now
for individual tissues.

01:16:23.071 --> 01:16:24.626
So where it started off,

01:16:24.626 --> 01:16:26.490
if you remember for the
human genome project,

01:16:26.490 --> 01:16:29.190
the equivalent is sort of,
the next level was kind of,

01:16:30.228 --> 01:16:31.690
you know, population genetics or GWAS,

01:16:31.690 --> 01:16:33.740
and it started off with individual genes

01:16:33.740 --> 01:16:35.490
rather than the whole human genome.

01:16:36.503 --> 01:16:37.336
And I would say for the Human Cell Atlas,

01:16:37.336 --> 01:16:41.680
the counterpart of that is
genetics of let's say blood

01:16:42.662 --> 01:16:45.343
or genetics of, let's say, you know,

01:16:47.340 --> 01:16:49.160
small intestinal biopsies,

01:16:49.160 --> 01:16:54.160
where you are able to gather
samples from hundreds of donors

01:16:55.046 --> 01:16:56.110
and can then calculate, like you said,

01:16:56.110 --> 01:16:57.950
differences in abundances of cell types

01:16:57.950 --> 01:17:00.920
or differences in patterns
of gene expression

01:17:00.920 --> 01:17:03.846
at the single cell level of,

01:17:03.846 --> 01:17:07.060
on the human genetic scale
using single-site EQTL

01:17:08.490 --> 01:17:10.873
or dynamic EQTL inference algorithms.

01:17:12.448 --> 01:17:14.640
And that's an area that's developing

01:17:14.640 --> 01:17:16.726
both on the experimental level

01:17:16.726 --> 01:17:17.945
and on the computational level,

01:17:17.945 --> 01:17:19.327
about what's the best way to do that.

01:17:19.327 --> 01:17:21.446
And in fact, we're collaborating
with Neil Lawrence,

01:17:21.446 --> 01:17:23.942
who's going to join me as a
discussant tomorrow about,

01:17:23.942 --> 01:17:27.040
in terms of algorithms for analyzing

01:17:27.040 --> 01:17:30.404
single cell genomics data
at the human genetics level,

01:17:30.404 --> 01:17:32.263
using Gaussian process
latent variable models.

01:17:34.629 --> 01:17:36.560
So I think that's where it's going, yeah.

01:17:36.560 --> 01:17:37.460
- [Wilson] Thanks.

01:17:39.787 --> 01:17:40.620
- Thanks.

01:17:40.620 --> 01:17:45.620
I guess we had, I guess,
Sunny, is that you?

01:17:48.746 --> 01:17:52.026
- So I think Fred was asking
a question, and he was saying.

01:17:52.026 --> 01:17:53.123
(cross talking with Priya and Sarah)

01:17:53.123 --> 01:17:56.543
- [Priya] We had one more
person, William, who had.

01:17:57.483 --> 01:17:58.316
- Yeah, and William, yep.

01:18:01.546 --> 01:18:02.960
- [William] Ah, hi, can you hear me?

01:18:02.960 --> 01:18:03.793
- Yep, hi.

01:18:06.035 --> 01:18:07.041
- Thanks for your talk.

01:18:07.041 --> 01:18:07.874
I really enjoyed it.

01:18:07.874 --> 01:18:10.603
I'm not a real scientist,
I'm a social scientist,

01:18:11.720 --> 01:18:13.577
so maybe, excuse my ignorance on this

01:18:14.944 --> 01:18:16.721
if it sounds kind of coming from

01:18:16.721 --> 01:18:18.260
like man on the street type perspective.

01:18:18.260 --> 01:18:20.870
But over the past year and a half,

01:18:20.870 --> 01:18:23.663
there seem to be certain countries

01:18:23.663 --> 01:18:26.162
that I wouldn't necessarily say are immune

01:18:26.162 --> 01:18:29.010
that have had a lot greater
chance of controlling,

01:18:29.010 --> 01:18:33.020
spread and transmission of
like the COVID-19 virus.

01:18:33.020 --> 01:18:35.701
I was wondering if there's any evidence

01:18:35.701 --> 01:18:39.370
in sort of the genomic field
of whether certain populations

01:18:39.370 --> 01:18:44.370
might be like more genetically
adept at overcoming COVID

01:18:45.080 --> 01:18:47.360
than other say like in Southeast Asia,

01:18:47.360 --> 01:18:50.680
perhaps some previous exposure
to regional waves of SARS

01:18:51.810 --> 01:18:55.270
has given some sort of
advantageous adaptations

01:18:55.270 --> 01:18:59.773
to the body's dealing with this new virus.

01:19:05.038 --> 01:19:10.000
- So advantageous adaptations
from the viral point of view?

01:19:10.000 --> 01:19:12.910
Sorry, I don't quite
understand the question.

01:19:12.910 --> 01:19:13.910
- [William] Yeah, I
was wondering, I guess,

01:19:13.910 --> 01:19:17.619
I guess what I'm wondering is
if you looked at all this data

01:19:17.619 --> 01:19:18.722
that you've collected,

01:19:18.722 --> 01:19:21.800
if there's any indications
that you're seeing unfold

01:19:22.936 --> 01:19:25.040
from certain populations
in parts of the world

01:19:25.890 --> 01:19:27.330
are different in a way

01:19:27.330 --> 01:19:32.330
that allows a better
response to viral infections?

01:19:33.316 --> 01:19:35.336
- Oh, okay, no now I
understand what you mean.

01:19:35.336 --> 01:19:36.340
So, the answer to that is

01:19:36.340 --> 01:19:38.563
we don't have enough data to really,

01:19:40.330 --> 01:19:42.343
so the answer to that is,

01:19:43.381 --> 01:19:44.722
there're a couple of different answers.

01:19:44.722 --> 01:19:49.398
So in this Gaussian process
latent variable model approach

01:19:49.398 --> 01:19:51.690
that I mentioned, what we can
do as map the associations

01:19:51.690 --> 01:19:56.690
with immunity to COVID-19 onto both nasal

01:19:57.470 --> 01:19:59.140
sort of epithelial data,

01:19:59.140 --> 01:20:03.080
and also onto blood data of populations

01:20:03.080 --> 01:20:08.010
and show that the OAS1 mutation

01:20:08.010 --> 01:20:10.150
in this RNA processing enzyme

01:20:10.150 --> 01:20:14.500
that's part of the innate immune
response against the virus

01:20:14.500 --> 01:20:17.173
that's present in every
single cell in our body,

01:20:18.395 --> 01:20:22.850
that there are indeed
sort of genetic variants

01:20:22.850 --> 01:20:25.190
that lead to a higher expression

01:20:25.190 --> 01:20:28.440
with a certain splice
variant versus other variants

01:20:28.440 --> 01:20:31.410
that lead to a lower expression
with a lower splice variant.

01:20:31.410 --> 01:20:36.410
And that the ones with the mutation

01:20:36.499 --> 01:20:41.400
that leads to the splicing
truncation has lower expression.

01:20:41.400 --> 01:20:46.400
And that may explain the higher propensity

01:20:47.090 --> 01:20:51.023
to severe COVID-19 of people
who have that genetic variant.

01:20:52.919 --> 01:20:55.810
So essentially we can sort
of take the association data

01:20:55.810 --> 01:20:58.110
and interpret it in terms of expression

01:20:58.110 --> 01:20:59.210
based on these data sets.

01:20:59.210 --> 01:21:00.810
That's one thing that we can do.

01:21:01.998 --> 01:21:03.140
And that's one thing that we're showing

01:21:03.140 --> 01:21:06.839
in this unpublished work
that we'll post online soon.

01:21:06.839 --> 01:21:08.737
And then the other thing that we can learn

01:21:08.737 --> 01:21:11.332
by looking at population data of COVID-19

01:21:11.332 --> 01:21:16.332
is the difference between
children and adults

01:21:17.295 --> 01:21:18.750
in terms of their innate and
adaptive immune responses.

01:21:18.750 --> 01:21:22.360
And we have a post on that on
a publication on Med Archive

01:21:22.360 --> 01:21:23.610
that I didn't talk about.

01:21:24.498 --> 01:21:26.414
And what we see there is that the children

01:21:26.414 --> 01:21:28.810
have a much stronger
innate immune response

01:21:30.053 --> 01:21:33.992
and a more polyclonal,
adaptive immune response.

01:21:33.992 --> 01:21:37.478
So children are really
kind of better poised

01:21:37.478 --> 01:21:39.429
to get rid of the virus quickly.

01:21:39.429 --> 01:21:42.040
Whereas our innate immune
response is kind of more sluggish,

01:21:42.040 --> 01:21:43.443
our adult response is,

01:21:44.955 --> 01:21:46.520
and the kids are also developing

01:21:46.520 --> 01:21:50.200
a kind of TNB cell response
from scratch, if you will.

01:21:50.200 --> 01:21:52.770
So they don't rely on
their memory of TNB cells,

01:21:52.770 --> 01:21:54.640
which we do as adults.

01:21:54.640 --> 01:21:56.650
We rely on our immune memory.

01:21:56.650 --> 01:22:00.773
And so we have smaller,
larger clones of TNB cells.

01:22:00.773 --> 01:22:03.140
Whereas the kids are seeing
these viruses for the first time

01:22:03.140 --> 01:22:07.467
and have a much more kind
of diverse population

01:22:08.770 --> 01:22:10.463
of TNB cells in their response.

01:22:13.730 --> 01:22:16.610
So that's a kind of, those
are the bits of things

01:22:16.610 --> 01:22:19.059
that we've learned from this work.

01:22:19.059 --> 01:22:21.691
There's a lot more to
learn, but it's, you know,

01:22:21.691 --> 01:22:22.820
we have learned quite
a lot about the innate,

01:22:22.820 --> 01:22:26.360
about immune responses
in different genetic,

01:22:26.360 --> 01:22:28.260
with people with different
genetic variants,

01:22:28.260 --> 01:22:30.233
kids versus adults and so on.

01:22:31.316 --> 01:22:34.558
And we'll continue to study
this over the coming years

01:22:34.558 --> 01:22:36.530
because it's, you know,
incredibly important,

01:22:36.530 --> 01:22:39.760
I think not just for COVID-19
actually, but, you know,

01:22:39.760 --> 01:22:40.990
we're actually learning new things

01:22:40.990 --> 01:22:43.393
about virology immunity overall.

01:22:45.120 --> 01:22:47.030
- So there's a question by Fred.

01:22:47.030 --> 01:22:48.553
Let's take that question.

01:22:49.437 --> 01:22:52.600
- Yep, so Fred is asking,

01:22:52.600 --> 01:22:54.795
is there something in common

01:22:54.795 --> 01:22:56.257
between the three parts of the talk?

01:22:56.257 --> 01:23:00.093
And I would say the central
take home message is really,

01:23:01.472 --> 01:23:02.910
the central thing that
I wanted to illustrate

01:23:02.910 --> 01:23:05.680
as a common thread
throughout all these things

01:23:05.680 --> 01:23:08.060
is really the iterative cycle

01:23:08.060 --> 01:23:11.580
between having big data in biology,

01:23:11.580 --> 01:23:15.790
using statistical computational
machine learning approaches

01:23:15.790 --> 01:23:18.830
to analyze it and make predictions,

01:23:18.830 --> 01:23:21.660
and then going back to
the cycle of experiments

01:23:21.660 --> 01:23:23.260
to validate those predictions.

01:23:23.260 --> 01:23:25.690
Like I said, in biology,
we have the luxury

01:23:25.690 --> 01:23:27.620
of being able to do that cycle

01:23:27.620 --> 01:23:29.600
because we can interrogate molecular

01:23:29.600 --> 01:23:31.830
and cellular systems experimentally.

01:23:31.830 --> 01:23:34.200
It's not like the cosmos or the climate.

01:23:34.200 --> 01:23:37.260
And really the central message
that I wanna get across

01:23:37.260 --> 01:23:40.750
is that that's a very powerful paradigm

01:23:40.750 --> 01:23:43.810
of iterating from biology to prediction

01:23:43.810 --> 01:23:45.220
with these big data methods,

01:23:45.220 --> 01:23:46.933
and then back to experiments.

01:23:52.180 --> 01:23:55.910
You know, and that holds
across structural biology.

01:23:55.910 --> 01:23:58.770
It holds across cellular biology.

01:23:58.770 --> 01:24:01.573
It holds across tissue
biology and virology.

01:24:03.324 --> 01:24:06.586
It's whatever you, wherever
you look kind of thing.

01:24:06.586 --> 01:24:08.180
- Yeah, and I think, as
you mentioned, Sarah,

01:24:08.180 --> 01:24:12.080
this ability to do controlled experiments

01:24:12.080 --> 01:24:16.826
is really key in validating
these predictions

01:24:16.826 --> 01:24:21.826
for the kinds of conceptual
computational models

01:24:21.943 --> 01:24:24.370
that are being built.

01:24:24.370 --> 01:24:28.563
So if there are no further questions,

01:24:29.886 --> 01:24:31.900
just wait for a minute to see
if anyone raises their hand

01:24:31.900 --> 01:24:35.120
or types in the chat.

01:24:35.120 --> 01:24:38.240
Let's all thank Sarah
for an excellent talk.

01:24:38.240 --> 01:24:41.823
- Yes, thank you everybody
for staying here so late.

01:24:42.750 --> 01:24:45.192
- [Priya] Thank you very much, yeah.

01:24:45.192 --> 01:24:46.025
- Very much. Yeah.

